window.SA_BANK = [{"id":"intervals","title":"Intervals: What, Why and How","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"What's the smallest interval on a keyboard, from one key to the very next?","a":"a half step","w":["a whole step","a 2nd","an octave"],"why":"One key to the next, black or white, is a half step."},{"q":"C up to G spans how many letter names, counting C as 1?","a":"5, so it's a 5th","w":["7, so it's a 7th","4, so it's a 4th","8, so it's an octave"],"why":"Size comes from counting letters: C D E F G is five, a 5th. (It's 7 half steps.)"},{"q":"Which part of an interval's name comes from counting half steps?","a":"the quality (major, minor, perfect…)","w":["the size (2nd, 3rd…)","neither","both"],"why":"Letters give the size; half steps give the quality."},{"q":"A major 3rd made one half step smaller (same letters) becomes…","a":"a minor 3rd","w":["a diminished 3rd","a perfect 3rd","an augmented 2nd"],"why":"Major made smaller is minor. Minor made smaller again would be diminished."},{"q":"Which intervals belong to the perfect family?","a":"unisons, 4ths, 5ths and octaves","w":["2nds, 3rds, 6ths and 7ths","only 5ths","3rds and 6ths"],"why":"Unisons, 4ths, 5ths and octaves are perfect, diminished or augmented."},{"q":"C–D♯ and C–E♭ sound the same. Why do they have different names?","a":"They span different numbers of letters","w":["One is higher than the other","One uses a black key","They don't; they're both minor 3rds"],"why":"C–D♯ spans two letters (an augmented 2nd); C–E♭ spans three (a minor 3rd)."},{"q":"On the white keys, which 5th is not perfect?","a":"B up to F","w":["C up to G","E up to B","D up to A"],"why":"B–F is a diminished 5th. Every other white-key 5th is perfect."},{"q":"A song is moved up 3 half steps to suit a singer. What happens to its intervals?","a":"They stay exactly the same","w":["They all get 3 half steps bigger","They flip direction","They become minor"],"why":"Transposition changes every note but keeps every interval."},{"q":"A motive leaps up a 5th. In its melodic inversion, it…","a":"leaps down a 5th","w":["leaps up a 4th","leaps up an octave","plays the notes backwards"],"why":"Melodic inversion turns every up into a down."},{"q":"After a wide leap up, what does a melody usually do?","a":"come back down, often by step","w":["leap up again","stop","repeat the top note"],"why":"Most tunes balance a big leap with stepwise motion the other way."},{"q":"Which stack makes a major triad, from the bottom up?","a":"a major 3rd, then a minor 3rd","w":["a minor 3rd, then a major 3rd","two major 3rds","two minor 3rds"],"why":"C–E is a major 3rd and E–G a minor 3rd: C major."},{"q":"How many half steps are there from C up to G?","a":"7","w":["5","4","8"],"why":"C4 → G4 is 7 half steps: a perfect 5th."},{"q":"C up to E is a 3rd. How many half steps make it a major 3rd?","a":"4","w":["3","5","2"],"why":"Four half steps across three letters: a major 3rd."},{"q":"A perfect 5th made a half step smaller (same letters) becomes…","a":"diminished","w":["minor","augmented","major"],"why":"Perfect intervals shrink to diminished and grow to augmented."},{"q":"A minor 3rd made a half step smaller (same letters) becomes…","a":"diminished","w":["major","perfect","augmented"],"why":"In the major/minor family, minor made smaller is diminished."},{"q":"D♯ and E♭ are the same piano key. They're called…","a":"enharmonic","w":["parallel","relative","inverted"],"why":"Enharmonic notes sound the same but are spelled differently."},{"q":"Why does each degree of a major or minor scale form a specific interval with the tonic?","a":"the scale uses each letter name once","w":["the scale uses only white keys","every scale step is a whole step","the tonic is always C"],"why":"Using each letter once means each degree is a definite 2nd, 3rd, 4th…"},{"q":"Stripped down, a melody is…","a":"a chain of intervals","w":["a list of chords","a set of rhythms only","a key signature"],"why":"Each hop from note to note is an interval."},{"q":"Why do you still recognize a song after it's transposed?","a":"every interval stays the same","w":["every note stays the same","the rhythm changes","the key signature stays the same"],"why":"Transposition moves every note by the same amount, keeping the intervals."},{"q":"Why are big leaps used carefully in vocal melodies?","a":"they're harder to tune and they get noticed","w":["they're impossible to sing","they always sound dissonant","they change the key"],"why":"Steps sit easily in the voice; leaps are dramatic."},{"q":"A melody stretches up with an octave leap, then heads back toward its home note. What usually brings it home?","a":"smaller and smaller intervals","w":["another octave leap","repeating the top note","it never comes back"],"why":"It falls back toward the home note by shrinking intervals."},{"q":"Lower the middle note of a major triad by a half step and you get…","a":"a minor triad","w":["an augmented triad","a diminished triad","a seventh chord"],"why":"The bright major chord turns dark: minor."},{"q":"Why is it useful to hear a note as, say, the 5th above the tonic?","a":"it tells you where you are in the key","w":["it tells you the tempo","it tells you the meter","it tells you the instrument"],"why":"Every note in a key sits some interval above the tonic."},{"q":"Without keys, some 20th-century composers built chords and pieces from…","a":"intervals: stacks of 4ths, half-step clusters, twelve-note rows","w":["only major triads","the circle of fifths","key signatures"],"why":"With no scale to organize the notes, intervals became the building blocks."}]},{"id":"introduction-to-pitch","title":"Introduction to Pitch","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"Why does a theremin's pitch change when the player's hand moves toward the rod?","a":"The body forms a capacitor with the antenna, and changing that distance shifts an oscillator's frequency","w":["The hand blocks sound waves coming from the rod","The rod vibrates faster when it gets warm","The hand presses a hidden key"],"why":"Hand and antenna form a capacitor; a change in capacitance retunes the variable oscillator."},{"q":"Near the theremin's pitch antenna, a small hand movement…","a":"has a bigger effect than it would farther away","w":["has a smaller effect","affects only the volume","does nothing"],"why":"The pitch field is uneven: it gets steeper close to the rod, so players place notes by ear."},{"q":"Most orchestras and bands tune the A above middle C (A4) to about…","a":"440 Hz","w":["415 Hz","261 Hz","1,000 Hz"],"why":"A4 = 440 Hz is the usual standard. Some orchestras go a little higher (442–443); Baroque ensembles often use 415, about a semitone lower."},{"q":"220 Hz to 440 Hz is an octave. What frequency is an octave above 440 Hz?","a":"880 Hz","w":["660 Hz","452 Hz","1,320 Hz"],"why":"An octave doubles the frequency: 440 × 2 = 880."},{"q":"Which frequency ratio makes a perfect 5th?","a":"3 : 2","w":["2 : 1","4 : 3","5 : 4"],"why":"3 : 2 is the perfect 5th. 2 : 1 is the octave, 4 : 3 the perfect 4th, 5 : 4 the major 3rd."},{"q":"How many cents are in one equal-tempered semitone?","a":"100","w":["12","50","1,200"],"why":"1,200 ÷ 12 = 100 cents."},{"q":"A violinist tunes against an A and hears the beats getting slower. What's happening?","a":"The two pitches are converging","w":["The pitches are moving further apart","The string is growing louder","The overtones are fading out"],"why":"As the difference shrinks, the beats slow down, and they stop when the pitches match."},{"q":"A string's fundamental is 100 Hz. Its 3rd partial vibrates at…","a":"300 Hz","w":["103 Hz","33 Hz","150 Hz"],"why":"Partial 3 vibrates 3 times as fast: 3 × 100 = 300 Hz."},{"q":"What mostly decides whether a note sounds like a flute or an oboe?","a":"the balance of its overtones","w":["its fundamental frequency","how loudly it's played","the key it's in"],"why":"Both can play the same pitch. The oboe's strong upper partials give it a different timbre from the flute's weak ones."},{"q":"Speed up a click past about how many per second and you start to hear a pitch?","a":"20","w":["2","200","2,000"],"why":"Around 20 per second the clicks fuse, and the pitch you hear is the click rate."},{"q":"What does each key on a touch-tone phone send?","a":"two pitches at once, one low and one high","w":["one pitch for each digit","as many clicks as the digit","a short melody"],"why":"Each row has a low tone and each column a high tone; a key sends its row's tone and its column's tone together, a dyad."},{"q":"Absolute (perfect) pitch is the ability to…","a":"name or sing a note with no reference tone","w":["sing perfectly in tune","hear the interval between two notes","tune an instrument by listening for beats"],"why":"Absolute pitch is labeling. Hearing notes by their relationships is relative pitch."},{"q":"Which change raises a string's pitch?","a":"making it shorter or tighter","w":["making it longer","making it heavier","loosening the tuning peg"],"why":"Shorter, tighter or lighter strings vibrate faster, so they sound higher."},{"q":"Which synthesizer waveform is a pure tone with no overtones?","a":"sine","w":["square","sawtooth","triangle"],"why":"A sine wave has no overtones. Triangle and square have odd harmonics; sawtooth has all of them."},{"q":"What is frequency measured in?","a":"hertz (Hz): vibrations per second","w":["decibels","beats per minute","cents"],"why":"Pitch depends on frequency: cycles per second, or hertz."},{"q":"Roughly what range of frequencies can people hear?","a":"about 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz","w":["about 200 to 2,000 Hz","about 1 to 100 Hz","about 20,000 to 40,000 Hz"],"why":"Human hearing spans roughly 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz (less at the top with age)."},{"q":"Which sounds like a bigger step: 110 → 220 Hz or 440 → 880 Hz?","a":"they sound the same: both are octaves","w":["110 → 220","440 → 880","neither is an octave"],"why":"Both double the frequency, so both are octaves."},{"q":"Which frequency ratio makes a perfect 4th?","a":"4 : 3","w":["3 : 2","2 : 1","5 : 4"],"why":"Simple ratios make consonances: 2:1 octave, 3:2 fifth, 4:3 fourth."},{"q":"Two tones at 440 and 443 Hz beat how many times a second?","a":"3","w":["443","1","883"],"why":"Beats per second equal the difference in frequency."},{"q":"What does the audio chorus effect imitate?","a":"many slightly different singers or players sounding together","w":["an echo in a large hall","a single pure tone","a sudden key change"],"why":"Slow beating between slightly detuned copies sounds rich, like a choir."},{"q":"What are overtones also called?","a":"harmonics or upper partials","w":["fundamentals","formants","beats"],"why":"The overtones above the fundamental are harmonics or upper partials."},{"q":"A string vibrating in halves produces which partial?","a":"the 2nd: twice the fundamental, an octave higher","w":["the fundamental","the 3rd partial","no partial at all"],"why":"Vibrating in halves gives 2× the frequency: the octave above."},{"q":"Two pulse streams in a 3 : 2 ratio, sped up into the pitch range, become…","a":"a perfect 5th","w":["an octave","a major 3rd","a tritone"],"why":"3:2 is the ratio of the perfect 5th."},{"q":"How did a rotary phone dial a 5?","a":"by sending five clicks down the line","w":["by sending two tones at once","by sending a single high tone","by sending a short melody"],"why":"Rotary dials sent one click per number (ten for 0)."},{"q":"Why do touch-tone frequencies avoid simple ratios like 2:1 and 3:2?","a":"so voices and music on the line don't fake a key","w":["to make the tones sound pleasant","because phones can't play octaves","to save electricity"],"why":"Harmonically unrelated pairs are unlikely to come from a voice."},{"q":"How is a Shepard tone built?","a":"a stack of sine waves an octave apart, loudest in the middle of the range","w":["one sine wave sweeping upward","a chord of major triads","white noise filtered by a resonator"],"why":"The top octaves fade out as new ones fade in at the bottom."},{"q":"What makes the Shepard scale seem to rise forever?","a":"after twelve semitone steps the stack returns to where it started","w":["each step gets louder","the tempo speeds up","the tones change instrument"],"why":"Every step sounds higher, yet the cycle repeats."},{"q":"Relative pitch is the ability to…","a":"hear and name the relationships between notes","w":["name any note with no reference","sing louder than others","hear frequencies above 20,000 Hz"],"why":"Relative pitch hears intervals and relationships; absolute pitch names notes."},{"q":"Some studies find absolute pitch more often among speakers of…","a":"tone languages such as Mandarin or Cantonese","w":["languages with long words","Latin-based languages","languages with no vowels"],"why":"In tone languages, pitch carries meaning."},{"q":"What sets the voice's pitch?","a":"the vibration rate of the vocal folds in the larynx","w":["the shape of the mouth","the size of the lungs","the nose"],"why":"The folds are the source; the throat, mouth and nose shape the tone."},{"q":"What are formants?","a":"overtone regions boosted by the shape of the throat, mouth and nose","w":["the lowest notes of a voice","the vocal folds","beats between two singers"],"why":"Formants shape vowels and tone color."},{"q":"Which waveform has all harmonics and sounds bright and buzzy?","a":"sawtooth","w":["sine","triangle","square"],"why":"Sawtooth contains every harmonic; square and triangle only odd ones."},{"q":"Subtractive synthesis starts with a rich waveform and…","a":"filters some of its overtones away","w":["adds more oscillators","speeds it up","turns it into a sine"],"why":"A filter removes overtones to shape the tone color."}]},{"id":"roman-numeral-analysis","title":"Introduction to Roman Numeral Analysis","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"In G major, which Roman numeral is the E minor chord?","a":"{rn:vi}","w":["{rn:VI}","{rn:iii}","{rn:ii}"],"why":"E is the 6th degree of G major, and the chord is minor: vi."},{"q":"In any major key, which chord is diminished?","a":"the chord on the 7th degree","w":["the chord on the 2nd degree","the chord on the 5th degree","none of them"],"why":"vii° is the only diminished triad in a major key."},{"q":"G7 is {rn:V7} in C major. What is it in D major?","a":"{rn:IV7}","w":["{rn:V7}","{rn:ii7}","{rn:VII7}"],"why":"G is the 4th degree of D major."},{"q":"What does {rn:V6} mean?","a":"the V chord in first inversion","w":["a V chord with an added 6th","the chord on the 6th degree","V played six times"],"why":"The 6 shows first inversion: the chord's 3rd is in the bass."},{"q":"How is a fully diminished seventh chord on the leading tone written?","a":"{rn:vii°7}","w":["{rn:viiø7}","{rn:VII7}","{rn:V7}"],"why":"vii°7 is fully diminished; viiø7 is half-diminished."},{"q":"In C minor, the chord on the 7th degree with its natural 7th (B♭) is VII. With the raised 7th (B♮) it's…","a":"{rn:vii°}","w":["{rn:VII}","{rn:♭VII}","{rn:v}"],"why":"B–D–F is diminished: vii°. VII is the B♭ major triad."},{"q":"You transpose I – vi – IV – V from C major to A major. What happens to the numerals?","a":"They stay the same","w":["They all move up a step","They all change to lowercase","They need new accidentals"],"why":"Only the letter names change; the numerals don't."},{"q":"In C major, what is A7?","a":"{rn:V7/ii}","w":["{rn:V7/V}","{rn:VI7}","{rn:V7/vi}"],"why":"A7 is the dominant 7th of D minor (ii): V7/ii."},{"q":"Which label goes with the Neapolitan chord?","a":"{rn:♭II6}","w":["{rn:ii6}","{rn:♭VII}","{rn:It+6}"],"why":"♭II6: D♭/F in C major."},{"q":"In classical analysis, {rn:I7} means…","a":"a major 7th chord on the tonic","w":["a dominant 7th on the tonic","a tonic triad in first inversion","a chord on the 7th degree"],"why":"In C major, I7 is C–E–G–B. A dominant 7th on the tonic would be written V7/IV."},{"q":"In a Nashville chart in C, what does 6- mean?","a":"an A minor chord","w":["the 6th bar","an A major chord","a chord on the lowered 6th"],"why":"6 is A in C major, and the minus makes it minor: Am."},{"q":"What does a Roman numeral name?","a":"the scale degree of the chord's root in the current key","w":["the bass note","the highest note","the chord's letter name"],"why":"Roman numerals label chords by where their roots sit in the key."},{"q":"In C major, which chords are major?","a":"I, IV and V","w":["ii, iii and vi","I, ii and V","IV, V and vi"],"why":"In major: I, IV, V major; ii, iii, vi minor; vii° diminished."},{"q":"What does a Roman numeral tell you that a chord symbol doesn't?","a":"its function: what the harmony does in the key","w":["which notes to play","the rhythm","the voicing"],"why":"G7 is V7 in C, IV7 in D, ♭VII7 in A: the numeral shows function."},{"q":"G7 is ♭VII7 in which major key?","a":"A major","w":["C major","D major","G major"],"why":"G sits on the lowered 7th degree of A."},{"q":"What does V7 (a plain 7) mean?","a":"a major triad with a minor 7th (a dominant seventh)","w":["a major triad with a major 7th","a minor seventh chord","a seventh chord in third inversion"],"why":"V7 is the dominant seventh: major triad + minor 7th."},{"q":"Which figure shows a seventh chord in first inversion?","a":"6/5","w":["6","4/3","4/2"],"why":"ii⁶₅ is ii⁷ in first inversion."},{"q":"In C minor, relative to the minor scale, how is the chord on E♭ labeled?","a":"{rn:III}","w":["{rn:♭III}","{rn:iii}","{rn:♭iii}"],"why":"In the minor-scale system, an accidental appears only when a root is altered from minor."},{"q":"In the major-based system for minor keys, the chord on C minor's 6th degree (A♭) is labeled…","a":"{rn:♭VI}","w":["{rn:VI}","{rn:vi}","{rn:♯VI}"],"why":"Relative to major, the lowered 6th degree gets a ♭."},{"q":"What changes when you transpose a progression?","a":"only the letter names","w":["the Roman numerals","the chord qualities","the distances from the tonic"],"why":"Every root keeps its distance from the tonic, so the numerals stay."},{"q":"How do you read V7/V aloud?","a":"“five-seven of five”","w":["“five-seven over five”","“five-seven divided by five”","“five-seven and five”"],"why":"V7/V is the dominant seventh of the dominant."},{"q":"In C major, what is E7?","a":"{rn:V7/vi}","w":["{rn:V7/iii}","{rn:III7}","{rn:vii°7}"],"why":"E7 resolves to A minor (vi): V7/vi."},{"q":"Why is there no V/I?","a":"that's just V","w":["it's too dissonant","the tonic can't be tonicized","it would be a Neapolitan"],"why":"The dominant of the tonic is simply V."},{"q":"In C major, D♭/F is labeled…","a":"{rn:♭II6} (the Neapolitan)","w":["{rn:ii6}","{rn:V/V}","{rn:♭VII}"],"why":"D♭ major in first inversion is the Neapolitan sixth."},{"q":"How is ii–V–I in C written in jazz (all-uppercase) style?","a":"IIm7 – V7 – I△7","w":["ii7 – V7 – I7","2m – 5 – 1","ii – V – I"],"why":"Jazz uses uppercase numerals plus suffixes like m7 and △7."},{"q":"What is the Nashville Number System?","a":"a chart shorthand naming chords by scale degree with plain digits","w":["a way of tuning guitars","a set of country lyrics","a type of figured bass"],"why":"One Nashville chart works in every key."},{"q":"Who is usually credited with developing the Nashville Number System?","a":"Neal Matthews Jr. of the Jordanaires","w":["Charlie McCoy","Chet Atkins","Johnny Cash"],"why":"Matthews developed it; players like Charlie McCoy spread it."},{"q":"In the Groove Maker, what happens to the numerals when you change the key?","a":"they stay put while the letter names shift","w":["they all shift","they become chord symbols","they disappear"],"why":"Numerals describe relationships, which don't change with the key."}]},{"id":"modes","title":"Modes: The Simple Truth","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"Play the white keys from D to D. Which mode is that?","a":"D Dorian","w":["D Ionian","D Aeolian","D Phrygian"],"why":"Starting on the 2nd degree of the major scale gives Dorian."},{"q":"Which mode starts on the 5th degree of a major scale?","a":"Mixolydian","w":["Lydian","Aeolian","Locrian"],"why":"5 → Mixolydian (G to G on the white keys)."},{"q":"Which modes are in the major family (major 3rd above the tonic)?","a":"Lydian, Ionian, Mixolydian","w":["Dorian, Aeolian, Phrygian","Lydian, Dorian, Locrian","Ionian, Aeolian, Locrian"],"why":"Lydian (♯4), Ionian, Mixolydian (♭7) have a major 3rd. The others have a minor 3rd."},{"q":"Lydian is major with which note changed?","a":"a raised 4th","w":["a lowered 7th","a lowered 2nd","a raised 6th"],"why":"Lydian = major with ♯4."},{"q":"Phrygian is natural minor with which note changed?","a":"a lowered 2nd","w":["a raised 6th","a raised 4th","a lowered 5th"],"why":"Phrygian = minor with ♭2."},{"q":"In PADMIL order, which mode comes right after Dorian?","a":"Mixolydian","w":["Aeolian","Ionian","Lydian"],"why":"P-A-D-M-I-L: after D comes M."},{"q":"What key signature does A Dorian use?","a":"1 sharp","w":["3 sharps","no sharps or flats","2 flats"],"why":"A major has 3 sharps; two flats more cancels two of them: 1 sharp (F♯)."},{"q":"What key signature does E Lydian use?","a":"5 sharps","w":["4 sharps","3 sharps","1 sharp"],"why":"E major has 4 sharps; Lydian adds one: 5."},{"q":"Which scale has an augmented 2nd between neighboring notes?","a":"harmonic minor","w":["Dorian","major pentatonic","Mixolydian"],"why":"Raising the 7th of natural minor opens a +2 between the 6th and 7th."},{"q":"What does the blues scale add to minor pentatonic?","a":"♯4 / ♭5","w":["♭2","a major 3rd","♯6"],"why":"Minor pentatonic plus ♯4/♭5 is the blues scale."},{"q":"How many notes does an octatonic scale have?","a":"8","w":["6","7","12"],"why":"Octatonic: 8 notes. Whole-tone: 6."},{"q":"What do relative modes have in common?","a":"the same notes, starting on a different tonic","w":["the same tonic, with different notes","the same key signature and tonic","nothing: they're unrelated"],"why":"Relative modes share a collection (like C major's white keys) and differ in tonic."},{"q":"The white keys from E to E make which mode?","a":"Phrygian","w":["Aeolian","Dorian","Mixolydian"],"why":"Starting on degree 3 of the major scale gives Phrygian."},{"q":"What do parallel modes share?","a":"the same tonic","w":["the same notes","the same key signature","the same pattern of steps"],"why":"Parallel modes keep the tonic and change the notes."},{"q":"Compared with major, Mixolydian changes which note?","a":"it lowers the 7th","w":["it raises the 4th","it lowers the 3rd","it lowers the 2nd"],"why":"Mixolydian is major with ♭7."},{"q":"Dorian is natural minor with which note changed?","a":"a raised 6th","w":["a lowered 2nd","a raised 7th","a raised 4th"],"why":"Dorian = minor ♯6."},{"q":"Which mode has both a lowered 2nd and a lowered 5th compared with natural minor?","a":"Locrian","w":["Phrygian","Aeolian","Dorian"],"why":"Locrian is minor ♭2 ♭5."},{"q":"A rock song's riff uses a major scale with a lowered 7th. Which mode is that?","a":"Mixolydian","w":["Lydian","Dorian","Phrygian"],"why":"A ♭7 in a major context marks Mixolydian, very common in rock."},{"q":"Ionian and Aeolian are the modal names for…","a":"major and natural minor","w":["harmonic and melodic minor","Lydian and Locrian","pentatonic and blues"],"why":"Ionian = major; Aeolian = natural minor."},{"q":"Moving one step to the right in PADMIL…","a":"raises one scale degree by a half step","w":["lowers the tonic","adds a flat","changes the tonic"],"why":"Each step right raises one degree: one more sharp or one fewer flat."},{"q":"Where does Locrian fit in the PADMIL lineup?","a":"on the far left, one note darker than Phrygian","w":["between Dorian and Mixolydian","on the far right, after Lydian","it doesn't fit at all"],"why":"Locrian is Phrygian with a lowered 5th: one step beyond the dark end."},{"q":"D Mixolydian uses which key signature?","a":"one sharp (D major's two, plus one flat)","w":["two sharps","three sharps","no sharps or flats"],"why":"D major has 2♯; one flat more leaves 1♯."},{"q":"In real modal pieces, what do composers often do with the key signature?","a":"keep the ordinary major or minor signature and add accidentals","w":["always write the exact modal signature","leave the key signature out","use only sharps"],"why":"Many modal pieces use the major or minor signature plus accidentals."},{"q":"What are the three steps for naming a mode by ear?","a":"find the tonic, hear whether the 3rd is major or minor, find the note that differs","w":["count the sharps, find the bass, name the chord","sing the scale down, then up, then in thirds","find the highest note, the lowest note and the tempo"],"why":"Tonic, then the 3rd (major or minor family), then the one changed note."},{"q":"In common-practice music, the augmented 2nd appears only in…","a":"harmonic minor, between 6 and 7","w":["melodic minor, between 6 and 7","the major scale, between 3 and 4","Dorian, between 5 and 6"],"why":"Harmonic minor's raised 7th leaves a three-half-step gap above 6."},{"q":"Pentatonic scales have how many half steps?","a":"none: only whole steps and minor 3rds","w":["two","one","five"],"why":"Pentatonic scales avoid half steps entirely."},{"q":"How are major and minor pentatonic related?","a":"they're relatives: same notes, different tonic","w":["they're parallel: same tonic, different notes","minor pentatonic has six notes","they share nothing"],"why":"The black keys make both E♭ minor pentatonic and G♭ major pentatonic."},{"q":"Why is the tonic ambiguous in whole-tone and octatonic scales?","a":"every note has the same surroundings","w":["they have no 5ths at all","they're always played fast","they use only black keys"],"why":"Symmetrical scales look the same from every note, so no note stands out as home."},{"q":"How many different whole-tone collections exist?","a":"2","w":["3","6","12"],"why":"The second shift brings the first collection back."}]},{"id":"more-about-intervals","title":"More About Intervals","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"Which group lists only perfect consonances?","a":"unisons, octaves and 5ths","w":["3rds and 6ths","2nds and 7ths","4ths and tritones"],"why":"Unison, octave and 5th are the perfect consonances. 3rds and 6ths are imperfect consonances."},{"q":"Why does a ⁶₄ chord usually resolve?","a":"A 4th above the bass is treated as a dissonance","w":["It has no 3rd","It contains a tritone","It's always out of tune"],"why":"On its own a 4th sounds open, but in common-practice harmony a 4th above the bass counts as dissonant."},{"q":"A pianist plays C and D at the same time. That's a…","a":"harmonic 2nd, heard as a clash","w":["melodic 2nd, heard as a step","harmonic 3rd","melodic 9th"],"why":"Played together, the 2nd is harmonic, and a harmonic 2nd sounds like a clash. One after the other, it would just be a step."},{"q":"E up to F is…","a":"a half step and a minor 2nd","w":["a whole step and a major 2nd","a half step and an augmented unison","a whole step and a minor 2nd"],"why":"One key apart (half step), with two different letter names (a 2nd)."},{"q":"A melody's high points, spread over four bars, are C, B, A and G. What do they form?","a":"a hidden line descending by step","w":["an octave leap","a chord","a repeated note"],"why":"Spread across four bars, the peaks walk down a scale."},{"q":"What simple interval is inside a 10th?","a":"a 3rd","w":["a 2nd","a 4th","a 5th"],"why":"10 − 7 = 3. The octave's two notes share one count."},{"q":"In a C9 chord, the D is the 9th. If the pianist plays it right next to C, what is it called in the chord symbol?","a":"still the 9th","w":["the 2nd","the 3rd","the 11th"],"why":"It stays the 9th wherever the pianist puts it."},{"q":"Which intervals above the tonic are the same in every major and minor scale?","a":"major 2nd, perfect 4th, perfect 5th","w":["major 3rd, major 6th, major 7th","minor 3rd, perfect 5th, minor 7th","perfect 4th, minor 6th, major 7th"],"why":"Every major and minor scale has M2, P4 and P5 above its tonic. The 3rd, 6th and 7th vary."},{"q":"Which interval above the tonic decides whether a scale is major or minor?","a":"the 3rd","w":["the 2nd","the 5th","the octave"],"why":"Major scales have a major 3rd above the tonic; minor scales have a minor 3rd."},{"q":"A minor 6th inverts to a…","a":"major 3rd","w":["minor 3rd","major 6th","perfect 4th"],"why":"6 + 3 = 9, and minor becomes major."},{"q":"An augmented 4th inverts to a…","a":"diminished 5th","w":["augmented 5th","perfect 5th","diminished 4th"],"why":"4 + 5 = 9, and augmented becomes diminished. Both are 6 half steps."},{"q":"C up to B♭ is hard to count. Use inversion: B♭ up to C is a major 2nd. So C up to B♭ is a…","a":"minor 7th","w":["major 7th","minor 2nd","diminished 7th"],"why":"The inversion of a major 2nd is a minor 7th."},{"q":"Which intervals are the imperfect consonances?","a":"3rds and 6ths","w":["4ths and 5ths","2nds and 7ths","unisons and octaves"],"why":"3rds and 6ths are stable but warmer than the perfect consonances."},{"q":"Which interval is a dissonance?","a":"the tritone","w":["the octave","the major 6th","the perfect 5th"],"why":"2nds, 7ths and the tritone are the dissonances."},{"q":"How is the perfect 4th treated in common-practice harmony?","a":"as a dissonance when it's above the bass","w":["always as a perfect consonance","always as an imperfect consonance","as an octave"],"why":"On its own it sounds open, but a 4th above the bass is treated as dissonant."},{"q":"A singer moves from G up to B. That's…","a":"a melodic major 3rd","w":["a harmonic major 3rd","a melodic minor 3rd","a harmonic minor 3rd"],"why":"Sung in turn, it's a melodic interval; G–B is a major 3rd."},{"q":"Does an interval's name change between melodic and harmonic?","a":"no: C–E is a major 3rd either way","w":["yes: harmonic intervals are one size larger","yes: melodic intervals are always minor","only for 2nds"],"why":"The name stays; consonance and dissonance are felt more strongly in harmony."},{"q":"C up to C♯ is a half step. What is the interval called?","a":"an augmented unison","w":["a minor 2nd","a diminished 2nd","a major 2nd"],"why":"Same letter, a half step apart: an augmented unison."},{"q":"Is every half step a minor 2nd?","a":"no: C to C♯, on a single letter, is an augmented unison","w":["yes, always","only in minor keys","only on black keys"],"why":"Every minor 2nd is a half step, but not every half step is a minor 2nd."},{"q":"A melody leaps up an octave from low C, and later leaps up from the same C by a 6th. How does the second reach compare with the first?","a":"it falls a 3rd short of the first","w":["it lands on the same high note","it goes higher","it moves in the opposite direction"],"why":"A 6th is a 3rd smaller than an octave, so the second high note is lower."},{"q":"To find the simple interval inside a compound one, you subtract…","a":"7","w":["8","12","2"],"why":"A 10th minus 7 is a 3rd; a 9th minus 7 is a 2nd."},{"q":"An 11th is a compound…","a":"4th","w":["3rd","5th","6th"],"why":"11 − 7 = 4: the 11th is a 4th plus an octave."},{"q":"In a scale like C–D–E–F–G–A–B, what intervals above the tonic do the 3rd, 6th and 7th make?","a":"major 3rd, major 6th, major 7th","w":["minor 3rd, minor 6th, minor 7th","major 3rd, minor 6th, minor 7th","perfect 3rd, perfect 6th, perfect 7th"],"why":"C major: E is M3, A is M6, B is M7."},{"q":"Inverted interval numbers always add up to…","a":"nine","w":["eight","seven","twelve"],"why":"A 2nd inverts to a 7th, a 3rd to a 6th: 2 + 7 = 3 + 6 = 9."},{"q":"A perfect 5th inverts to a…","a":"perfect 4th","w":["perfect 5th","diminished 4th","major 4th"],"why":"5 inverts to 4 (rule of nine), and perfect stays perfect."},{"q":"A major 2nd inverts to a…","a":"minor 7th","w":["major 7th","minor 6th","major 6th"],"why":"2 + 7 = 9, and major becomes minor."}]},{"id":"cadences","title":"Understanding Cadences","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"A line stops on a secondary dominant, but the melody runs straight on into the next line. Is that a cadence?","a":"no: the melody has to pause for a phrase to end","w":["yes: any dominant 7th makes one","yes: a half cadence","only if the chord is in root position"],"why":"Only phrase endings are cadences. A chord the melody passes through is just a point along the way."},{"q":"A phrase in E major ends E/B – B⁷ – E, with the melody on E. What is that?","a":"a perfect authentic cadence with a cadential six-four","w":["a plagal cadence","a half cadence","a deceptive cadence"],"why":"I⁶₄ – V⁷ – I, with the melody ending on the tonic: a PAC."},{"q":"What makes an authentic cadence perfect (PAC)?","a":"V and I both in root position, with the tonic in both outer voices","w":["any V–I at the end of a piece","a V⁷ instead of V","the soprano ending on the 3rd"],"why":"Root-position V and I, and the final I has its root in the bass and the soprano."},{"q":"A phrase ends V⁷ → I, both in root position, with scale degree 5 on top of the last chord. Which cadence is it?","a":"imperfect authentic (IAC)","w":["perfect authentic (PAC)","half (HC)","plagal (PC)"],"why":"Without the tonic on top, the authentic cadence is imperfect."},{"q":"A phrase ends on V. That's a…","a":"half cadence","w":["perfect authentic cadence","plagal cadence","deceptive cadence"],"why":"Ending on V is a half cadence (semicadence)."},{"q":"Which cadence is often called the “amen” cadence?","a":"plagal (IV–I)","w":["deceptive (V–vi)","half (I–V)","authentic (V–I)"],"why":"Subdominant to tonic, IV–I, is the plagal or “amen” cadence."},{"q":"Why does a deceptive cadence give only weak closure?","a":"We expect I after V and get a substitute, usually vi","w":["It ends on V","It has no dominant","It's in a minor key"],"why":"V sets up I, and the cadence swaps in vi instead."},{"q":"In a pop loop like I–V–vi–IV, where does each phrase end?","a":"open, on IV","w":["closed, on I","on V, a half cadence","on vi, a deceptive cadence"],"why":"The phrase lands on IV (a plagal half cadence), even though the next cycle starts on I."},{"q":"Which group lists only open cadences?","a":"half, plagal half, secondary half","w":["deceptive, imperfect authentic","authentic, plagal","perfect authentic, half"],"why":"Open: half, plagal half, secondary half. Weak: deceptive, IAC. Closed: authentic, plagal."},{"q":"In a balanced four-bar phrase, where does the cadence most often land?","a":"on the downbeat of bar 4","w":["on beat 1 of bar 1","in the middle of bar 2","on the last beat of bar 4"],"why":"The fourth-downbeat cadence is the most typical."},{"q":"Chords that keep changing after the cadence, before the next phrase, are called a…","a":"turnaround","w":["elision","pickup","pedal"],"why":"Post-cadential motion is a turnaround."},{"q":"A phrase in C major ends on C⁷, and the next phrase starts on F. What is that ending?","a":"a half cadence on a secondary dominant (V⁷/IV)","w":["a perfect authentic cadence","a plagal cadence","a deceptive cadence"],"why":"C⁷ is V⁷ of F (IV), and the next phrase goes there. Stopping on it is an open, secondary half cadence."},{"q":"Where do cadences happen?","a":"only at the ends of phrases (or at an elision)","w":["at every V–I progression","on every downbeat","only at the end of a piece"],"why":"A V–I in the middle of a phrase is just a progression; a cadence closes a phrase."},{"q":"Which final chords give a cadence the most closure?","a":"the tonic (or a tonic substitute)","w":["the dominant","the subdominant","the leading-tone chord"],"why":"Endings on the tonic are the most closed; endings on V or IV the weakest."},{"q":"V⁷–I, both in root position, but the soprano ends on the 3rd of I. Which cadence?","a":"imperfect authentic (IAC)","w":["perfect authentic (PAC)","half cadence","deceptive cadence"],"why":"A PAC needs the tonic in both outer voices; with the 3rd on top it's imperfect."},{"q":"vii°⁶ going to I at the end of a phrase is…","a":"an imperfect authentic cadence","w":["a perfect authentic cadence","a plagal cadence","a half cadence"],"why":"An inverted dominant-function chord makes the authentic cadence imperfect."},{"q":"Besides a pre-dominant chord, what often comes right before the V of a half cadence?","a":"a cadential ⁶₄","w":["a deceptive vi","a ♭VII","a secondary half cadence"],"why":"Half cadences are often approached by a pre-dominant or a cadential ⁶₄."},{"q":"A phrase that stops on IV can be called a…","a":"plagal half cadence","w":["plagal cadence","deceptive cadence","imperfect authentic cadence"],"why":"Ending on IV is open, like a half cadence: a plagal half cadence (PHC)."},{"q":"V⁷ to vi at the end of a phrase is a…","a":"deceptive cadence","w":["plagal cadence","half cadence","imperfect authentic cadence"],"why":"V going to a tonic substitute, usually vi, is a deceptive cadence."},{"q":"In a minor key, the deceptive cadence usually goes from V to…","a":"VI","w":["iv","III","i"],"why":"In minor the tonic substitute is VI."},{"q":"What makes a chord like ♭VII cadential in a pop song?","a":"the melody and the phrase arrive on it, usually on the last strong downbeat","w":["it is always followed by I","it is in root position","it is borrowed from minor"],"why":"Any non-tonic chord can be an open cadential arrival if the phrase lands on it."},{"q":"How is Vsus4 different from an ordinary half cadence on V?","a":"the 4th replaces the leading tone, so it's open without the leading tone's pull","w":["it's closed, like an authentic cadence","it ends on the tonic","it adds a 7th to V"],"why":"The suspended 4th takes the place of the leading tone (the 3rd of V)."},{"q":"Which pair are both closed cadences?","a":"perfect authentic and plagal","w":["half and plagal half","deceptive and imperfect authentic","half and deceptive"],"why":"PAC and plagal are closed; deceptive and IAC are weak; half cadences are open."},{"q":"What usually answers an open phrase?","a":"one that ends with a stronger cadence","w":["another open one","one with no cadence at all","a change of meter"],"why":"An open (half) cadence is answered by a more conclusive one."},{"q":"A cadence on the third downbeat of a four-bar phrase gives…","a":"partial, provisional closure","w":["the strongest possible closure","no closure at all","a deceptive cadence"],"why":"The fourth downbeat is the typical cadence point; the third gives provisional closure."},{"q":"When one phrase ends just as the next one begins, that's…","a":"an elision","w":["a turnaround","a fermata","a half cadence"],"why":"At an elision the cadence and the next phrase's start share the same moment."},{"q":"A phrase in A minor ends G – C, with G acting as the dominant of C. What kind of ending is that?","a":"an authentic cadence on III: weakly closed","w":["a perfect authentic cadence on the tonic","a half cadence","a plagal cadence"],"why":"G – C is V – I in C, but C is III in A minor, so the phrase closes away from the tonic."},{"q":"Why does a deceptive cadence count as only weakly closed?","a":"V resolves, but not to the tonic","w":["it stops on V","it has no dominant","it only happens in minor"],"why":"V moves to a stable chord, usually vi (VI in minor), and the return home is put off."}]},{"id":"chord-figures-and-symbols","title":"Understanding Chord Figures and Symbols","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"A triad with a major 3rd above its root and a 5th a half step larger than perfect (C–E–G♯) is…","a":"augmented","w":["major","diminished","minor"],"why":"Major 3rd + augmented 5th (M3 + M3) is augmented: C–E–G♯."},{"q":"C–E♭–G♭–B♭ is a…","a":"half-diminished seventh","w":["fully diminished seventh","minor seventh","dominant seventh"],"why":"Diminished triad + minor 7th = half-diminished (Cø7). With B𝄫 it would be fully diminished."},{"q":"In any major key, what kind of seventh chord is built on the 5th degree?","a":"dominant seventh","w":["major seventh","minor seventh","half-diminished seventh"],"why":"V⁷ in major is always a dominant seventh (major triad + minor 7th)."},{"q":"G in the bass, with C and E above. What is the chord and its position?","a":"C major, second inversion","w":["C major, root position","G major, root position","E minor, first inversion"],"why":"Stacked: C–E–G, so the root is C. The 5th (G) is in the bass: second inversion."},{"q":"What figure shows a seventh chord in first inversion?","a":"6/5","w":["7","4/3","4/2"],"why":"Root position 7, first inversion 6/5, second 4/3, third 4/2."},{"q":"A triad in first inversion is figured…","a":"6","w":["6/4","7","nothing"],"why":"Root position: no figure. First inversion: 6. Second inversion: 6/4."},{"q":"In a figured bass, what does ♯6 under a bass note mean?","a":"raise the note a 6th above it by a half step","w":["raise the 3rd above it","raise the lowest note","play a 6th chord in a sharp key"],"why":"♯6 raises the 6th above the bass. An accidental alone applies to the 3rd."},{"q":"What does C/E mean?","a":"a C chord with E in the bass","w":["a C chord followed by an E chord","C and E played alone","an E chord with C in the bass"],"why":"C/E is C major in first inversion."},{"q":"On a lead sheet, how long does a chord symbol last?","a":"until another one appears","w":["one beat","one bar, always","until the end of the line"],"why":"A chord lasts until the next symbol, however many beats that is."},{"q":"What notes make up Gsus4?","a":"G–C–D","w":["G–B–D","G–A–D","G–B♭–D"],"why":"Root, perfect 4th, perfect 5th: G–C–D."},{"q":"In an inverted triad, a quick way to find the root is…","a":"the upper note of the 4th","w":["the lowest note","the highest note","the lower note of the 3rd"],"why":"An inverted triad contains a 4th, and its upper note is the root (in E–G–C, G–C is the 4th; C is the root)."},{"q":"Which triad is built from two minor 3rds?","a":"diminished","w":["minor","augmented","major"],"why":"m3 + m3 gives a diminished 5th: a diminished triad."},{"q":"On the staff, a root-position triad looks like…","a":"a snowman: line–line–line or space–space–space","w":["alternating lines and spaces","three notes on the same line","a ladder of seconds"],"why":"Stacked 3rds put all three notes on lines or all on spaces."},{"q":"C–E–G–B♭ is a…","a":"dominant seventh","w":["major seventh","minor seventh","half-diminished seventh"],"why":"A major triad with a minor 7th is a dominant (Mm) seventh."},{"q":"In a minor key, the seventh chord on the raised 7th degree (the leading tone) is…","a":"fully diminished","w":["half-diminished","dominant","minor seventh"],"why":"In minor, vii°⁷ on the leading tone is fully diminished."},{"q":"What decides a chord's inversion?","a":"which chord member is in the bass","w":["which note is on top","how the upper notes are spaced","the key signature"],"why":"Only the bass note sets the inversion; the upper notes don't matter."},{"q":"A seventh chord with its 7th in the bass is in…","a":"third inversion","w":["second inversion","first inversion","root position"],"why":"Bass on the 7th is third inversion, which only seventh chords have."},{"q":"G7 with D in the bass is figured…","a":"4/3","w":["6/5","4/2","7"],"why":"Second-inversion seventh chords are figured 4/3."},{"q":"When you read figures, a 10th above the bass counts as…","a":"a 3rd","w":["a 10th","a 5th","an octave"],"why":"Figures ignore octaves: a 10th is a 3rd, a 13th a 6th."},{"q":"A slash through a figure (or a + beside it) means…","a":"raise that interval a half step","w":["lower that interval a half step","leave that note out","double that note"],"why":"A slashed figure raises that interval by a half step."},{"q":"In A minor, a ♯ alone under the bass note E means…","a":"raise the 3rd above E: G becomes G♯","w":["raise E to E♯","raise the 6th above E","play an E minor chord"],"why":"A lone accidental affects the 3rd above the bass: E–G♯–B, the major dominant."},{"q":"What is D/C?","a":"a D major triad over a C bass","w":["a C chord with D in the bass","a D chord in second inversion","a C major triad with an added D"],"why":"A slash chord puts the bass note after the slash, even a note outside the chord."},{"q":"G7/F is G7 in which position?","a":"third inversion (4/2)","w":["second inversion (4/3)","first inversion (6/5)","root position (7)"],"why":"The 7th in the bass is third inversion, figured 4/2."},{"q":"On a lead sheet, who works out the bass line and the voicings?","a":"the players, from the chord symbols","w":["the composer, in small notes","the figured bass","the lyricist"],"why":"Everything beyond melody, lyrics and chord symbols is left to the players."},{"q":"Why does a sus4 chord sound open and unsettled?","a":"it has no 3rd, so it's neither major nor minor","w":["it has no 5th","it adds a 7th","it's always in second inversion"],"why":"The 4th replaces the 3rd, so the chord's quality is undecided."},{"q":"In classical music, where does the 4–3 suspension most often appear?","a":"on the dominant, right before a cadence","w":["on the tonic at the start of a phrase","on the leading-tone chord","only in minor keys"],"why":"The 4–3 over the dominant before a cadence is the classic spot."},{"q":"B in the bass, D–F–A above. What is it?","a":"Bø7 in root position","w":["B° in first inversion","Bm7 in root position","D minor in second inversion"],"why":"B–D–F–A is a half-diminished seventh on B, with B in the bass: root position."}]},{"id":"reading-pitch","title":"Understanding Clefs and Pitch Reading","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"Going from a line to the very next space is…","a":"a step: the following letter","w":["a skip: every other letter","an octave","no change of pitch"],"why":"Line to the next space (or space to the next line) is one step, the next letter."},{"q":"The bass clef's two dots surround which note?","a":"F3, the F below middle C","w":["G4","middle C","F4"],"why":"The F clef's dots surround its 4th line, F3."},{"q":"Where is middle C on the grand staff?","a":"on a ledger line between the two staves","w":["on the treble staff's bottom line","in the bass staff's top space","on the middle line of the treble staff"],"why":"Middle C sits on its own ledger line between the staves and can be written from either one."},{"q":"Which instrument reads alto clef?","a":"viola","w":["violin","flute","piano"],"why":"The viola reads alto clef. Cello, bassoon and trombone sometimes use tenor clef."},{"q":"Who is credited with the staff of lines a 3rd apart, around the year 1000?","a":"Guido of Arezzo","w":["J. S. Bach","Arnold Schoenberg","Johann Maelzel"],"why":"Guido of Arezzo is credited with the staff; his letter clefs became ours."},{"q":"F♯ appears early in a bar. Later in the same bar there's another F on the same line with no sign. What is it?","a":"F♯","w":["F♮","F♭","It depends on the next bar"],"why":"The sharp carries through the bar for that line or space. The bar line cancels it."},{"q":"C𝄪 (C double sharp) sounds the same as…","a":"D","w":["C♯","E","B"],"why":"C up two semitones is D."},{"q":"In guitar tab, what does a 0 on a line mean?","a":"play that string open","w":["rest","the lowest note on the guitar","fret 10"],"why":"0 means the open string; 3 means press behind the 3rd fret."},{"q":"In a DAW piano roll, what does a note's vertical position show?","a":"its pitch","w":["its loudness","its length","which track it's on"],"why":"Up and down is pitch, one row per semitone; length is duration."},{"q":"Going from a line to the next line up is…","a":"a skip (a 3rd)","w":["a step","an octave","a 5th"],"why":"Line to line (or space to space) skips a letter: a 3rd."},{"q":"Which octave number is middle C?","a":"C4","w":["C3","C5","C1"],"why":"Middle C is C4; octave numbers change at each C."},{"q":"The treble clef's curl circles which line?","a":"the 2nd line, G4","w":["the 3rd line, B4","the 1st line, E4","the 4th line, D5"],"why":"The G clef marks G4 on the 2nd line."},{"q":"The spaces of the treble staff spell…","a":"F A C E","w":["E G B D","A C E G","G B D F"],"why":"Treble spaces, bottom to top: F A C E."},{"q":"The lines of the bass staff are…","a":"G B D F A","w":["E G B D F","A C E G B","F A C E G"],"why":"Bass lines, bottom to top: G B D F A."},{"q":"A note sits on the bottom line of the treble staff (E). The same line in bass clef is…","a":"G","w":["E","C","A"],"why":"Each bass-clef line or space is two letters later than in treble."},{"q":"What joins the two staves of a grand staff?","a":"a brace","w":["a tie","a double bar","a repeat sign"],"why":"A brace joins the treble and bass staves of the grand staff."},{"q":"The space just above the treble staff is a useful landmark. Which note is it?","a":"high G (G5)","w":["high F (F5)","high A (A5)","middle C"],"why":"High G sits in the space above the treble staff."},{"q":"How many lines does the great staff have?","a":"eleven","w":["ten","five","twelve"],"why":"Five + one + five: an eleven-line ladder of 3rds."},{"q":"The tenor clef is a C clef on which line?","a":"the 4th line","w":["the 3rd line","the 2nd line","the 5th line"],"why":"The C clef on the 3rd line is alto; on the 4th, tenor."},{"q":"Why do cellists, bassoonists and trombonists sometimes switch to tenor clef?","a":"to avoid ledger lines in higher passages","w":["to play louder","because their instruments transpose","to show a key change"],"why":"Tenor clef keeps high passages on the staff."},{"q":"Early chant notation (neumes) showed…","a":"the shape of a melody, but not exact pitches","w":["exact pitches and rhythms","only the lyrics","chord symbols"],"why":"Neumes showed contour; the staff and letter clefs added exact pitch."},{"q":"Where did our clef signs come from?","a":"letters (C, F, G) marking key lines","w":["pictures of instruments","Roman numerals","the first letters of singers' names"],"why":"The written C, F and G became today's stylized clefs."},{"q":"Which reading strategy uses notes you know instantly, like G4 and F3?","a":"landmarks","w":["intervals","memory devices","patterns"],"why":"Landmarks are anchor notes; you read others by step or skip from them."},{"q":"In a chord with a 2nd, where does the higher note go?","a":"to the right of the stem","w":["to the left of the stem","directly above the lower note","on a separate stem"],"why":"In a 2nd, the noteheads sit on opposite sides, the higher one on the right."},{"q":"Where does an accidental go?","a":"immediately before the notehead, on the same line or space","w":["just after the notehead","above the note","at the start of the bar"],"why":"Accidentals sit right before the note, centered on its line or space."},{"q":"What does a natural sign do?","a":"cancels a sharp or flat","w":["raises a note a semitone","lowers a note a semitone","doubles the note"],"why":"A natural cancels a sharp or flat from the key signature or earlier in the bar."},{"q":"An F♯4 appears in a bar. Does it change an F5 later in the same bar?","a":"no: it affects only that line or space (that octave)","w":["yes: it affects every F","only if they're tied","only in treble clef"],"why":"An accidental applies only to its own octave until the bar line."},{"q":"In text you write F♯. On the staff, where does the sharp go?","a":"before the note","w":["after the note","above the note","under the note"],"why":"In text the accidental follows the letter; on the staff it comes first."},{"q":"How many lines does bass tab have?","a":"four (G D A E, top to bottom)","w":["six","five","three"],"why":"Bass has four strings, so bass tab has four lines."},{"q":"In guitar tab, the top line is…","a":"the highest-sounding string (high E)","w":["the lowest string (low E)","the A string","the melody"],"why":"The top line is the high E string; the bottom line is low E."},{"q":"In a DAW, an audio clip's waveform shows…","a":"loudness over time, but not which notes","w":["the pitches of every note","the chord symbols","the key signature"],"why":"Only MIDI clips in the piano roll show pitch."}]},{"id":"keys-and-scale-degrees","title":"Understanding Keys, Scale Degrees and Tendency Tones","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"In E major, the lowered supertonic is the note F♮. How is it labeled as a scale degree?","a":"♭2","w":["♮2","♯1","2"],"why":"It's lowered, so ♭2, even though the actual note is F♮."},{"q":"What is scale degree 5 called?","a":"the dominant","w":["the subdominant","the mediant","the submediant"],"why":"1 tonic, 2 supertonic, 3 mediant, 4 subdominant, 5 dominant, 6 submediant, 7 leading tone (or subtonic)."},{"q":"What pitch is the dominant in G major?","a":"D","w":["C","F♯","E"],"why":"G A B C D: the 5th degree is D."},{"q":"What's the difference between the leading tone and the subtonic?","a":"The leading tone is a half step below the tonic; the subtonic is a whole step below","w":["They're the same note","The subtonic is a half step above the tonic","The leading tone appears only in major"],"why":"In minor, the 7th in the key signature is the subtonic (a whole step down). Raised, it becomes the leading tone (a half step down)."},{"q":"Where does the leading tone (ti) tend to go?","a":"up to do","w":["down to la","down to sol","up to re"],"why":"Ti resolves up to do, strongly, especially in V → I."},{"q":"Where does fa (the subdominant) tend to go?","a":"down to mi","w":["up to sol","up to la","down to re"],"why":"Fa falls a half step to mi."},{"q":"A melody's short phrases end 2→1, 4→3, 6→5 and 4→3. How does each phrase end?","a":"a tendency tone falls by step to a stable tone","w":["on the leading tone","with a leap up an octave","on scale degree 2"],"why":"Each phrase lands on a stable tone."},{"q":"In C major, a D♯ appears. Where does it most likely go?","a":"up to E","w":["down to D","down to C","up to F♯"],"why":"Raised notes tend upward: D♯ resolves up to E."},{"q":"A piece has one flat in the key signature, and there are C♯s scattered through it. What key is it most likely in?","a":"D minor","w":["F major","B♭ major","C major"],"why":"C♯ is the raised leading tone of D minor."},{"q":"What's one of the quickest and most reliable clues to the key?","a":"closure: how the passage begins and ends","w":["the number of notes in the melody","the time signature","the tempo marking"],"why":"Cadences and melodic resolution at the end (and the first chord at the start) usually reveal the tonic."},{"q":"What does a caret over a number (3̂) mean?","a":"a scale degree","w":["an accented note","a raised note","an interval size"],"why":"A caret marks a scale degree."},{"q":"In C major, F♯ is labeled as which scale degree?","a":"♯4","w":["♭5","4","♯5"],"why":"Altered degrees keep their number and take a ♯ or ♭."},{"q":"What is scale degree 4 called?","a":"the subdominant","w":["the submediant","the supertonic","the mediant"],"why":"Degree 4 is the subdominant."},{"q":"What pitch is the subdominant in C major?","a":"F","w":["G","A","E"],"why":"C D E F: the subdominant of C is F."},{"q":"What is scale degree 6 called?","a":"the submediant","w":["the subdominant","the mediant","the subtonic"],"why":"Degree 6 is the submediant."},{"q":"Which scale degrees are the stable tones?","a":"1, 3 and 5","w":["1, 4 and 5","2, 4 and 7","1, 2 and 3"],"why":"1, 3 and 5 are the anchor tones; the rest tend to move."},{"q":"Where does the 7th of a V⁷ chord tend to go?","a":"down by step","w":["up by step","up a 4th","it stays put"],"why":"Chordal 7ths resolve down by step (F → E in C major)."},{"q":"In major, la (the submediant) tends to move…","a":"down to sol, or up through ti to do","w":["straight up to do","down to mi","nowhere: it's stable"],"why":"La falls to sol, or rises la–ti–do (in minor it tends down)."},{"q":"In jazz, the 3rd and 7th of a dominant chord are called…","a":"guide tones","w":["anchor tones","passing tones","tendency triads"],"why":"Guide tones lead to the next chord's important tones."},{"q":"Chromatic notes usually move…","a":"in the direction of their inflection: raised notes up, lowered notes down","w":["always up","always down","by leap"],"why":"A raised note leans up, a lowered note leans down."},{"q":"A passage ends on a root-position triad. What does that suggest?","a":"that chord is probably the tonic","w":["that chord is the dominant","the passage modulates","the key is minor"],"why":"Ending on a root-position triad suggests the tonic; ending on a dominant 7th suggests the dominant."},{"q":"Listeners tend to hear the first chord of a passage as…","a":"the tonic, unless a stronger progression changes their mind","w":["the dominant","the subdominant","unimportant"],"why":"The opening chord is heard as home until stronger evidence appears."},{"q":"Why can a key signature mislead?","a":"music changes key, and some Baroque pieces use one flat fewer than the key needs","w":["key signatures are often printed wrong","they apply only to the first bar","they ignore the bass clef"],"why":"Modulation and older “modal” signatures can hide the real key."},{"q":"The conventional approach to finding a key gathers the pitches and filters out…","a":"non-chord tones","w":["chord tones","the bass line","all accidentals"],"why":"Remove non-chord tones, then see whether the rest form a diatonic collection."}]},{"id":"lead-sheets","title":"Understanding Lead Sheets","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"What three things does a lead sheet give?","a":"melody, lyrics and chord symbols","w":["melody, bass line and drum part","full piano part, lyrics and dynamics","chord symbols, tempo and instrumentation only"],"why":"Melody on one staff, lyrics under it, chord symbols above it."},{"q":"A bound collection of lead sheets is called a…","a":"fake book","w":["score","part book","hymnal"],"why":"A fake book is a whole library of lead sheets."},{"q":"Which of these does a lead sheet usually leave out?","a":"the pianist's voicings and rhythm","w":["the melody's rhythm","the lyrics","the form: repeats and endings"],"why":"Accompaniment, bass line, dynamics and instrumentation are the players' choices."},{"q":"A melody note inside a chord's region isn't in the chord. What is it most likely?","a":"a non-chord tone, such as a passing tone","w":["a mistake in the lead sheet","a sign to change chords","the bass note"],"why":"Most melody notes in a region belong to the chord; some pass between or decorate them."},{"q":"On a lead sheet, G7 is written on beat 3 while the singer is still holding C from beat 1. What does that show?","a":"The accompaniment can move on before the melody does","w":["The singer is singing a wrong note","G7 replaces the melody","Chords change only when the melody changes"],"why":"The chord starts on beat 3 regardless; the singer's held C sits over it."},{"q":"A song's chords change every two beats under long melody notes. That rate of chord change is the…","a":"harmonic rhythm","w":["tempo","meter","syncopation"],"why":"Harmonic rhythm is how often the chords change."},{"q":"The bass notes of Cm, G7/D, C7/E, F form…","a":"a rising line, C–D–E–F","w":["a falling line","a repeated C","an arpeggio of C minor"],"why":"The slash chords write a stepwise rising bass into the symbols."},{"q":"What kind of chord is Dm7(♭5)?","a":"half-diminished seventh","w":["minor seventh","dominant seventh","fully diminished seventh"],"why":"Dm7(♭5) = D–F–A♭–C, the same as Dø7."},{"q":"In the voice-and-piano version, how does the pianist use the chord symbols?","a":"bass notes on beats 1 and 3, harmony on 2 and 4","w":["plays the melody in octaves","plays only the bass notes","plays one chord per bar"],"why":"Left-hand bass on 1 and 3, right-hand chords on 2 and 4, all built from the symbols."},{"q":"Who usually reads from lead sheets?","a":"jazz, pop, folk and theater musicians, church and wedding bands","w":["only orchestral string players","only classical pianists","only singers in opera"],"why":"Lead sheets are how songs travel in popular, jazz, folk, theater and church music."},{"q":"Compared with a song's lead sheet, what does a recording of the song usually add?","a":"its own tempo, instruments and accompaniment","w":["a different melody and lyrics","new chord symbols","nothing: it plays the page exactly"],"why":"The performers supply the arrangement; the lead sheet gives only the song."},{"q":"Which of these does a lead sheet usually include?","a":"the form: repeats, endings and section letters","w":["the bass line note by note","dynamics and accents","the introduction and solos"],"why":"Form markings are included; accompaniment, dynamics and intros are left to the players."},{"q":"A word like “Medium Slow” or “Swing” at the top of a lead sheet gives…","a":"the tempo or style","w":["the key","the form","the instrumentation"],"why":"Lead sheets usually give a tempo or style word rather than a metronome mark."},{"q":"What does a chord symbol mark?","a":"where a region of harmony begins; it lasts until the next one","w":["one beat of a single chord","a note the singer must hold","the bass note only"],"why":"Each symbol starts a harmonic region on the beat where it's written."},{"q":"Can the accompaniment move to a new chord before the melody does?","a":"yes: the harmony can change under a held note","w":["no: chords change only when the melody moves","only at the end of a section","only in a slash chord"],"why":"Chord regions and melody notes don't have to change together."},{"q":"A song in C minor briefly uses C7/E, then an F chord with A♮. What do those two chords do?","a":"they brighten it for a moment: borrowed and chromatic harmony","w":["they confirm the minor key","they end the song","they change the meter"],"why":"Chromatic and borrowed chords stand out against the minor key."},{"q":"Fm6 is…","a":"a minor chord with an added 6th","w":["a minor chord in first inversion","an F chord with a 6th in the bass","a diminished chord"],"why":"In chord symbols, 6 means an added 6th above the root."},{"q":"In the voice-and-piano setting, which beats get the right hand's chords?","a":"2 and 4","w":["1 and 3","every beat","only beat 1"],"why":"Bass notes on 1 and 3, chords on 2 and 4: a boom-chuck pattern."},{"q":"Where did the pianist's accompaniment come from?","a":"entirely from the lead sheet's chord symbols","w":["from a full piano score","from the melody's rhythm only","from the original recording"],"why":"The accompaniment is built entirely from the chord symbols."}]},{"id":"major-and-minor-scales","title":"Understanding Major and Minor Scales","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"What is a scale's tonic?","a":"its home note, the one it's named after","w":["its highest note","the note a half step below the top","any note with a sharp"],"why":"The tonic is home base: the starting note and the scale's name."},{"q":"Between which white keys are the natural half steps?","a":"E–F and B–C","w":["C–D and F–G","D–E and A–B","G–A and C–D"],"why":"E–F and B–C are half steps; every other pair of white-key neighbors is a whole step."},{"q":"What is the major scale's pattern of steps?","a":"W W H W W W H","w":["W H W W H W W","W W W H W W H","H W W W H W W"],"why":"Major: W W H W W W H. (W H W W H W W is natural minor.)"},{"q":"A major scale is built from two tetrachords. What joins them?","a":"a whole step, between 4 and 5","w":["a half step, between 4 and 5","a half step, between 3 and 4","an octave"],"why":"Lower tetrachord 1–4, a whole step, upper tetrachord 5–8."},{"q":"F♯ is in the key signature, on the top line. An F in the lowest space comes along. What is it?","a":"F♯","w":["F♮","F♭","It depends on the octave"],"why":"Key-signature sharps and flats apply to that letter in every octave, for the whole piece."},{"q":"What is the order of the sharps in a key signature?","a":"F C G D A E B","w":["B E A D G C F","C G D A E B F","F B E A D G C"],"why":"Sharps: F C G D A E B. Flats are the same backwards."},{"q":"A key signature has three flats: B♭, E♭, A♭. What major key is it?","a":"E♭ major","w":["A♭ major","B♭ major","F major"],"why":"The next-to-last flat is E♭, so E♭ major (or its relative minor, C minor)."},{"q":"Going clockwise around the circle of fifths (up a 5th each time), each key has…","a":"one more sharp (or one fewer flat)","w":["one more flat","the same number of sharps","two more sharps"],"why":"Up a 5th adds a sharp: C (none), G (1), D (2), A (3)…"},{"q":"What is A major's relative minor?","a":"F♯ minor","w":["A minor","C♯ minor","D minor"],"why":"The 6th degree of A major is F♯, and F♯ minor shares A major's three sharps."},{"q":"Harmonic minor raises the 7th degree. Why?","a":"to make a leading tone, so V is major","w":["to avoid an augmented 2nd","to make it sound like major","to match the relative major"],"why":"The raised 7th is a half step below the tonic, so the V chord is major and pulls strongly to i. The cost is an augmented 2nd between 6 and 7."},{"q":"Melodic minor usually comes down as…","a":"natural minor","w":["harmonic minor","major","Dorian"],"why":"Ascending: raised 6 and 7. Descending: back to natural minor."},{"q":"In movable-do major, what syllable is scale degree 5?","a":"sol","w":["fa","la","mi"],"why":"do re mi fa sol: 5 is sol."},{"q":"In movable do, what does do mean?","a":"the tonic of whatever key you're in","w":["always the note C","always the lowest note","the leading tone"],"why":"Movable do names scale degrees; fixed do names pitches."},{"q":"What is the biggest difference between a major and a minor scale?","a":"the 3rd note: higher in major, lower in minor","w":["the first note","the number of letter names","the 5th note"],"why":"The 3rd is the one note that always differs between major and minor."},{"q":"A whole step is…","a":"two half steps","w":["one key to the very next","three half steps","the distance from C to E"],"why":"A whole step (tone) equals two half steps."},{"q":"In a major scale, the half steps fall between which degrees?","a":"3–4 and 7–8","w":["2–3 and 5–6","4–5 and 7–8","1–2 and 4–5"],"why":"The pattern puts its half steps after the 3rd and 7th degrees."},{"q":"In C major, which notes are a half step apart?","a":"E–F and B–C","w":["C–D and F–G","D–E and A–B","F–G and G–A"],"why":"E–F and B–C are the natural half steps, degrees 3–4 and 7–8."},{"q":"Where is a key signature written?","a":"after the clef at the start of every line","w":["only at the start of the piece","only in the first bar of each section","above the staff"],"why":"It's repeated on every staff, and only changed mid-line if the key changes."},{"q":"A key signature with no sharps or flats means…","a":"C major or A minor","w":["C major only","A major or F♯ minor","any key"],"why":"No sharps or flats: C major or its relative minor, A minor."},{"q":"What is the order of the flats?","a":"B E A D G C F","w":["F C G D A E B","B E A D C G F","E B A D G C F"],"why":"Flats go B E A D G C F, the reverse of the sharps."},{"q":"Which major key has four sharps?","a":"E major","w":["A major","B major","D major"],"why":"F♯ C♯ G♯ D♯: the last sharp is the leading tone, so the key is E."},{"q":"On the circle of fifths, going counterclockwise (down a 5th) adds…","a":"a flat","w":["a sharp","a natural","nothing"],"why":"Down a 5th each time adds one more flat."},{"q":"Which pair is enharmonic on the circle of fifths?","a":"F♯ and G♭","w":["F♯ and G","C and C♯","B and B♭"],"why":"Around the bottom the sides meet: B = C♭, F♯ = G♭, C♯ = D♭."},{"q":"What is the natural minor scale's pattern of steps?","a":"W H W W H W W","w":["W W H W W W H","W H W W W H W","H W W W H W W"],"why":"Natural minor uses the relative major's notes starting on its 6th degree."},{"q":"Parallel keys share…","a":"a tonic (like C major and C minor)","w":["a key signature","the same notes","a dominant only"],"why":"Parallel keys share a tonic; the minor has three more flats or three fewer sharps."},{"q":"What does harmonic minor's raised 7th create between degrees 6 and 7?","a":"an augmented 2nd","w":["a half step","a perfect 4th","a whole step"],"why":"The raised 7th leaves a gap of three half steps above 6: an augmented 2nd."},{"q":"Natural minor with only the 6th raised is…","a":"the Dorian mode","w":["harmonic minor","melodic minor descending","the major scale"],"why":"Raising just 6 gives Dorian: minor, with a bright 6th and no leading tone."},{"q":"Which intervals above the tonic are the same in major and every form of minor?","a":"the major 2nd, perfect 4th and perfect 5th","w":["the 3rd, 6th and 7th","only the octave","the major 3rd and major 7th"],"why":"The 2nd, 4th and 5th stay put; the 3rd, 6th and 7th differ."},{"q":"Which interval above the tonic is minor in every form of the minor scale?","a":"the 3rd","w":["the 6th","the 7th","the 2nd"],"why":"Harmonic and melodic minor raise 6 or 7, but the 3rd stays minor."},{"q":"In major, which syllable is the leading tone?","a":"ti","w":["la","te","do"],"why":"Do re mi fa sol la ti: ti is degree 7, the leading tone."},{"q":"In solfège, a lowered mi becomes…","a":"me","w":["mi","ma","ri"],"why":"Lowered syllables take e (me, le, te), except re, which becomes ra."},{"q":"In la-based minor, what syllable is the tonic?","a":"la","w":["do","mi","ti"],"why":"La-based minor starts on la; the raised 7th becomes si."},{"q":"In do-based minor, the 3rd, 6th and 7th degrees are sung…","a":"me, le, te","w":["mi, la, ti","ri, fi, si","ra, se, le"],"why":"Minor's lowered degrees take the lowered syllables me, le, te."},{"q":"In fixed do, what is do?","a":"always C, whatever the key","w":["the tonic of the key","the lowest note of the melody","always A"],"why":"Fixed do names notes: do is C, re is D, and so on."}]},{"id":"meter","title":"Understanding Meter and Beaming","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"Quadruple meter's pattern of strong and weak beats is…","a":"strong–weak–medium–weak","w":["strong–weak","strong–weak–weak","strong–strong–weak–weak"],"why":"Beat 3 of a quadruple bar is medium-strong."},{"q":"In 2/2 (cut time), which note gets the beat?","a":"the half note","w":["the quarter note","the whole note","the eighth note"],"why":"2/2 has two half-note beats."},{"q":"How many beats are in a bar of 12/8?","a":"4","w":["12","6","3"],"why":"12 ÷ 3 = 4 dotted-quarter beats: compound quadruple."},{"q":"Six eighth notes beamed 3 + 3. Which time signature fits?","a":"6/8","w":["3/4","2/4","3/8"],"why":"Beaming in threes shows two compound beats: 6/8. In 3/4 they'd be beamed 2 + 2 + 2."},{"q":"A bar of 7/8 beamed 2 + 2 + 3 has how many beats?","a":"3, and they aren't equal","w":["7, all equal","2, both equal","3, all equal"],"why":"Three beats: two short (2 eighths) and one long (3 eighths)."},{"q":"A piece goes 3/4, 5/8, 2/4, 7/8 as a regular feature. That's…","a":"mixed meter","w":["polymeter","hemiola","syncopation"],"why":"Changing time signatures as an ongoing feature is mixed meter. Polymeter is two meters at once."},{"q":"How should eighth notes be beamed in 6/8?","a":"in threes","w":["in pairs","in fours","all six together"],"why":"Each dotted-quarter beat holds three eighths, so beam in threes."},{"q":"In older vocal music, beams showed…","a":"the syllables of the text (joined notes shared one syllable: a melisma)","w":["the beat, as in instrumental music","the dynamics","which notes to breathe after"],"why":"Traditional vocal beaming followed the text. Modern editions usually beam by beat."},{"q":"A piece in 3/4 begins with a single quarter-note pick-up. Which bar is usually bar 1?","a":"the first complete bar","w":["the pick-up bar","the last bar","the bar after the first phrase"],"why":"Bar numbering usually starts with the first complete bar."},{"q":"An eighth–quarter–eighth rhythm puts the longest note on the offbeat. That's…","a":"syncopation","w":["hemiola","an anacrusis","compound meter"],"why":"Starting on an offbeat and sustaining into a stronger position is syncopation."},{"q":"Two bars of 3/4 heard as three half notes, just before a cadence, is a…","a":"hemiola","w":["syncopation","mixed meter","pick-up"],"why":"Six beats regrouped 3 × 2 instead of 2 × 3 is the classic hemiola."},{"q":"Triple meter's pattern of strong and weak beats is…","a":"strong–weak–weak","w":["strong–weak","strong–weak–medium–weak","weak–strong–weak"],"why":"Triple meter: one strong beat, then two weak ones."},{"q":"In simple meter, each beat divides into…","a":"two","w":["three","four","unequal groups"],"why":"Simple meter divides the beat in two; compound in three."},{"q":"A meter whose beats are unequal, mixing groups of two and three, is…","a":"asymmetrical (additive)","w":["compound","simple","mixed"],"why":"Asymmetrical meters combine beats of two and three divisions."},{"q":"In compound meter, how is a beat counted?","a":"1 & a","w":["1 & 2 &","1 e & a","1 2 3 4"],"why":"Each compound beat divides in three: 1 & a."},{"q":"In a simple time signature, what does the bottom number show?","a":"the note value that gets the beat","w":["the number of beats","the tempo","how many bars to play"],"why":"The top number counts beats; the bottom names the beat's note value."},{"q":"How many beats are in a bar of 6/8?","a":"2","w":["6","3","4"],"why":"6/8 is compound duple: two dotted-quarter beats."},{"q":"In compound meter, the beat is…","a":"a dotted note","w":["always a quarter note","an eighth note","a half note"],"why":"Compound beats are dotted values, like the dotted quarter in 6/8."},{"q":"A bar of 3/4 and a bar of 6/8 both hold six eighths. How do they differ?","a":"3/4 has three beats in twos; 6/8 has two beats in threes","w":["they're identical","6/8 is always faster","3/4 has six beats"],"why":"Same total, different grouping: 2 + 2 + 2 versus 3 + 3."},{"q":"12/8 often sounds like 4/4 with…","a":"a shuffle or triplet feel","w":["half as many beats","a faster tempo","no strong beats"],"why":"Four beats in both, but 12/8's beats divide in three."},{"q":"How can 5/8 be grouped?","a":"2 + 3 or 3 + 2","w":["only 2 + 2 + 1","5 equal beats","only 3 + 2"],"why":"Asymmetrical meters mix groups of two and three; the beaming shows which."},{"q":"A mixed-meter change falls at the start of a new line. What else do you write?","a":"a cautionary time signature at the end of the system before it","w":["nothing","a double bar","a repeat sign"],"why":"The cautionary time signature at the end of the line warns of the change."},{"q":"Two different meters sounding at the same time is…","a":"polymeter","w":["mixed meter","hemiola","syncopation"],"why":"Mixed meter changes over time; polymeter layers meters simultaneously."},{"q":"In 4/4, eighth notes may be beamed in fours, but never…","a":"across the middle of the bar (beats 2–3)","w":["in pairs","on beat 1","with a rest inside"],"why":"Beaming across beats 2–3 hides the middle of the bar."},{"q":"How are sixteenth notes beamed?","a":"by beat, four per quarter-note beat","w":["eight at a time","by half bar","one flag each"],"why":"Sixteenths are beamed by beat, with secondary beams showing subdivision."},{"q":"In vocal music, what joins the notes of a melisma?","a":"a slur","w":["a tie","a hyphen","an extender line"],"why":"A slur joins a melisma; hyphens and extenders belong to the lyrics."},{"q":"A word's last syllable is sung to a melisma. What follows it under the notes?","a":"an extender line","w":["a hyphen","a slur","nothing"],"why":"The extender line shows the final syllable is held through the melisma."},{"q":"Traditionally, a piece that starts with a pick-up ends with…","a":"a shortened final bar that completes the pick-up's bar","w":["an extra full bar","a fermata","a repeat"],"why":"The pick-up and the shortened last bar add up to one bar (though many pieces end with a full bar)."},{"q":"Syncopation puts the accent…","a":"between the beats, on a weak beat or offbeat sustained into a strong position","w":["only on beat 1","on every beat equally","only in compound meter"],"why":"A note starting off the beat and held into an accented spot shifts the accent."},{"q":"Alternating 6/8 and 3/4 creates an effect related to…","a":"hemiola","w":["syncopation","an anacrusis","mixed meter only"],"why":"6/8 (3 + 3) against 3/4 (2 + 2 + 2) is the hemiola's three-against-two."}]},{"id":"non-chord-tones","title":"Understanding Non-Chord Tones","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"Over a C major chord (C–E–G), which melody note is a non-chord tone?","a":"D","w":["C","E","G"],"why":"C, E and G are chord tones; D isn't."},{"q":"In a full score with no chord symbols, what should you work out first?","a":"the harmonic rhythm: where the harmony changes","w":["the melody's highest note","the tempo","the dynamics"],"why":"Find how often the chords change, then name each chord."},{"q":"In classical music, is the 7th of a chord usually a chord tone or a dissonance?","a":"a dissonance, except on the dominant","w":["always a chord tone","always a dissonance, even on V","a chord tone, as in jazz"],"why":"In classical and folk music, 7ths are usually dissonances except on V; in jazz they're treated as chord tones."},{"q":"An escape tone is…","a":"approached by step and left by leap","w":["approached by leap and left by step","approached and left by step","held over from the previous chord"],"why":"Escape tone: step in, leap out. Appoggiatura: leap in, step out."},{"q":"A non-chord tone that falls on the beat, while the chord is sounding, is…","a":"accented","w":["unaccented","a chord tone","a pedal point"],"why":"On the beat it's accented; between beats it's unaccented."},{"q":"A held-over dissonance (like a 4–3) unfolds in three phases. What are they?","a":"preparation, suspension, resolution","w":["approach, leap, step","attack, decay, release","tonic, dominant, tonic"],"why":"Prepared as a chord tone, held into a new chord (dissonant), then resolved down by step."},{"q":"A note held over from the previous chord that resolves up by step is a…","a":"retardation","w":["suspension","anticipation","pedal tone"],"why":"Held over and resolving up: retardation."},{"q":"Which suspension figure has the suspended note in the bass?","a":"2-3","w":["4-3","9-8","7-6"],"why":"In a 2-3 the bass is held over and resolves down, making a 2nd become a 3rd."},{"q":"A melody note arrives before its chord and is then repeated or held into it. That's…","a":"an anticipation","w":["a suspension","a retardation","an appoggiatura"],"why":"An anticipation is the next chord's note, heard early."},{"q":"According to the chart, which type is approached by the same note and left by the same note?","a":"pedal tone","w":["suspension","anticipation","neighbor tone"],"why":"A pedal tone is held (or repeated) straight through."},{"q":"What's another name for a non-chord tone?","a":"a dissonance","w":["a consonance","a chord tone","a root"],"why":"A pitch outside the current chord is a dissonance."},{"q":"Where do non-chord tones mostly appear?","a":"in the melody","w":["only in the bass","only in chords","only at cadences"],"why":"When they turn up in the accompaniment, there's usually melodic activity there."},{"q":"In a lead sheet, how do you find the non-chord tones?","a":"spell each chord and compare its notes with the melody","w":["look for the longest notes","circle every accidental","look for notes on the beat"],"why":"Any melody pitch that isn't in the current chord is a non-chord tone."},{"q":"What notes are in Fm7?","a":"F–A♭–C–E♭","w":["F–A–C–E♭","F–A♭–C–E","F–A♭–B–E♭"],"why":"Fm7 is F–A♭–C plus E♭."},{"q":"What is harmonic rhythm?","a":"how often the chords change","w":["the rhythm of the melody","the tempo","the number of beats in a bar"],"why":"Harmonic rhythm is the rate of chord change."},{"q":"Is something an incomplete iii chord or a V chord? In most cases…","a":"it's V","w":["it's iii","it's neither","it's a non-chord tone"],"why":"Progression types favor V over an incomplete iii."},{"q":"In jazz, sevenths and extensions are usually treated as…","a":"consonant chord tones","w":["dissonances","passing tones","suspensions"],"why":"Jazz treats 7ths and extensions as part of the chord."},{"q":"To label a non-chord tone, you sort it by…","a":"how it's approached and how it's left (step, leap or hold)","w":["how long it lasts","how loud it is","which octave it's in"],"why":"Each type is defined by its approach and its resolution."},{"q":"Two stepwise notes in a row filling in a 4th between notes of the harmony are a…","a":"double passing tone","w":["double neighbor","pedal tone","suspension chain"],"why":"Two consecutive passing tones fill in a larger gap."},{"q":"An appoggiatura is…","a":"approached by leap and left by step","w":["approached by step and left by leap","approached and left by step","held over from the previous chord"],"why":"The appoggiatura is the escape tone's mirror image."},{"q":"A note a step above and one a step below the resolution pitch, in a row, right before it, make a…","a":"double neighbor (changing tone)","w":["double passing tone","suspension","harmonic anticipation"],"why":"Neighbors on both sides of the resolution pitch form a double neighbor."},{"q":"An unaccented non-chord tone falls…","a":"between beats or on a weak part of the beat","w":["on the downbeat","only at cadences","in the bass"],"why":"Accented ones land on the beat; unaccented ones come after it."},{"q":"Most appoggiaturas leap up, then step down. Can one leap down and then step down too?","a":"yes: leaping in and stepping out is what counts","w":["no: that would be an escape tone","no: that would be a passing tone","only when it's in the bass"],"why":"The direction can match: what makes it an appoggiatura is the leap in and the step out."},{"q":"A pedal tone is…","a":"a note that persists from one harmony into the next","w":["a note that arrives early","a note that leaps away","the top note of a chord"],"why":"The pedal stays while the harmony changes, sometimes clashing with it."},{"q":"A low pedal tone sounds under a whole passage. When is it a non-chord tone?","a":"only under harmonies that don't contain it","w":["always","never: the bass is always part of the harmony","only on strong beats"],"why":"It clashes only where the harmony above leaves it out; the rest of the time it belongs."},{"q":"A 4-3 suspension…","a":"is a 4th above the bass, resolving down to a 3rd","w":["is a 3rd that rises to a 4th","has the suspension in the bass","resolves up by step"],"why":"Suspension figures count from the bass: 4 resolving to 3."},{"q":"How do you measure a suspension figure?","a":"count letter names from the bass up","w":["count half steps from the melody down","count from the tonic","count from the root of the next chord"],"why":"Suspension figures are intervals above the bass note."},{"q":"In a chain of 7-6 suspensions, each resolution…","a":"is held over to become the next preparation","w":["leaps away","returns to the 7th","becomes a pedal tone"],"why":"Each resolution is tied into the next bar and prepares the next suspension."},{"q":"The bass moves up a 5th just as a 4 resolves. The resolution is a 6th above the new bass. The label is…","a":"4-6","w":["4-3","7-6","9-8"],"why":"Measured from the moving bass, the resolution is a 6th: a 4-6."},{"q":"A harmonic anticipation…","a":"anticipates a note of the next chord, then moves to a different note of that chord","w":["is held over from the previous chord","is approached and left by step","stays the same through two chords"],"why":"It anticipates the next harmony, but not the note the melody actually lands on."},{"q":"According to the chart, how is a pedal tone approached and left?","a":"by the same note on both sides","w":["by step on both sides","by leap, then step","by step, then leap"],"why":"A pedal holds (or repeats) the same note into and out of the clash."}]},{"id":"rhythm-notation","title":"Understanding Rhythm Notation","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"In compound time, when may a single note value cross a beat?","a":"only when it spans complete pulses, like a dotted half in 6/8","w":["whenever the rhythm is simple","never, under any circumstances","only in the last bar"],"why":"In compound time no value should cross the beat, except combined whole beats (a dotted half filling both beats of 6/8, say)."},{"q":"In 3/4, how many of the three beats must the notation show?","a":"two","w":["one","all three","none, if the rhythm is simple"],"why":"Three-beat meters must show two out of three beats."},{"q":"Where should dotted notes go?","a":"on the front side of strong beats","w":["on weak beats","only at the end of a bar","after a rest"],"why":"A dot on beat 1 or 3 of 4/4 is clear; a dot on beat 2 hides beat 3."},{"q":"The left hand plays steady quarters in a piano piece. Can the right hand ignore the beat-showing rules?","a":"No: each staff has to make the meter clear by itself","w":["Yes, the left hand shows the beat","Yes, if the right hand has a melody","Only in slow pieces"],"why":"Follow the rules in keyboard music too, even when the other hand shows the beat."},{"q":"What's the difference between a tie and a slur?","a":"A tie joins two notes of the same pitch; a slur joins different pitches","w":["A tie is curved; a slur is straight","A slur joins the same pitch; a tie joins different pitches","There's no difference"],"why":"A tie makes one sustained note from two of the same pitch. A slur asks for different pitches played unbroken."},{"q":"All the stems point down. Where does the slur go?","a":"above, on the notehead side","w":["below, on the stem side","through the stems","either side"],"why":"Stems down, slur above. Stems up, slur below. Mixed stems, above."},{"q":"Where does a time signature appear in a part?","a":"at the start, and again only where the meter changes","w":["at the start of every line","in every bar","only at the end"],"why":"Time signatures appear only at the start unless the meter changes."},{"q":"A meter change falls at the start of a new line. What else do you write?","a":"a cautionary time signature at the end of the system before it","w":["nothing else","a double bar line on the new line","the old time signature again on the new line"],"why":"The cautionary time signature at the end of the line warns of the change."},{"q":"Steady eighths played long–short in jazz is called…","a":"swing","w":["hemiola","unmeasured rhythm","polymeter"],"why":"Swing, shuffle and notes inégales all play written equal eighths as unequal long–short pairs."},{"q":"With the same pulse, 4/4 against 3/4 lines up again every…","a":"12 beats","w":["7 beats","4 beats","24 beats"],"why":"Polymeter: the bars realign after 12 beats (3 bars of 4/4, 4 bars of 3/4)."},{"q":"Several notes sung on one syllable get…","a":"a slur, plus an extender at the end of a word","w":["a hyphen under every note","a new syllable for each note","a tie between every note"],"why":"A melisma is slurred, and an extender line continues to its last note."},{"q":"How many beats are in a bar of 6/8?","a":"2","w":["6","3","4"],"why":"6/8 has two dotted-quarter beats."},{"q":"In 3/4, how many beats does a quarter-note triplet occupy?","a":"2","w":["1","3","1½"],"why":"Three quarter-note triplets fill two quarter beats."},{"q":"Values sustained across a beat or half-bar must…","a":"keep the meter visible to the reader","w":["always be dotted","be written as rests","be beamed together"],"why":"Notation should reveal the meter's basic stresses."},{"q":"Why shouldn't you break beams for each syllable in vocal music?","a":"it hides the beat","w":["it makes the lyrics too long","it changes the meter","singers can't read flags"],"why":"Beaming by beat shows the meter; per-syllable flags hide it."},{"q":"An extended syncopation is fine as long as…","a":"the strong beats stay visible","w":["it's written with dots","it lasts less than a beat","it's in compound meter"],"why":"Syncopation is welcome; hiding the strong beats isn't."},{"q":"Six eighths beamed 2 + 2 + 2 suggest which meter?","a":"3/4","w":["6/8","2/4","9/8"],"why":"3/4 beams by quarter (2 + 2 + 2); 6/8 by dotted quarter (3 + 3)."},{"q":"With stems up, where does a tie go?","a":"below the notes","w":["above the notes","between the stems","on the stems"],"why":"Ties curve on the notehead side: stems up, tie below."},{"q":"Where does a meter change in the middle of a line get its time signature?","a":"right at the bar where it takes effect","w":["at the start of the line","at the end of the line","only in the first bar"],"why":"A mid-line change gets its time signature in place; only line-start changes need a cautionary signature."},{"q":"Which bar lines are always present?","a":"the final one on each staff row","w":["only the last bar line of the piece","only double bar lines","none: they're optional"],"why":"Every line of music ends with a bar line."},{"q":"One or more notes before the first downbeat of a phrase are an…","a":"anacrusis (pickup)","w":["syncopation","hemiola","agogic accent"],"why":"An anacrusis leads into the first downbeat."},{"q":"3 + 3 + 2 eighths in 4/4 is an example of…","a":"an uneven grouping within an even meter","w":["compound meter","mixed meter","a hemiola"],"why":"Uneven beaming can group an even meter asymmetrically."},{"q":"Two or more simultaneous patterns that don't share the same beat subdivision are…","a":"a polyrhythm","w":["polymeter","a metric modulation","mixed meter"],"why":"Polyrhythms align only at the start of each cycle."},{"q":"Reinterpreting a rhythmic value to change the tempo, notated like “♩ = ♩.”, is…","a":"metric modulation","w":["polymeter","hemiola","rubato"],"why":"Metric modulation reinterprets one value as another."},{"q":"In vocal music, where do dynamics go?","a":"above the staff, to avoid the lyrics","w":["below the lyrics","inside the staff","only at the start"],"why":"Lyrics go below the staff, so dynamics go above."},{"q":"What separates the syllables of a word in lyrics?","a":"hyphens","w":["spaces","slurs","commas"],"why":"Hyphens split syllables; spaces separate words."}]},{"id":"texture","title":"Understanding Texture","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"A solo flute plays a tune, and then a clarinet doubles it an octave lower. What's the texture now?","a":"still monophony","w":["polyphony","homophony","heterophony"],"why":"There's still only one melodic line, so it's monophony."},{"q":"Which of these is the oldest and most widespread texture?","a":"monophony","w":["homophony","polyphony","heterophony"],"why":"The earliest notated Western music is monophonic, and single-line singing is found everywhere."},{"q":"Fiddlers play the same tune together, each adding their own ornaments. That's…","a":"heterophony","w":["polyphony","monophony","homophony"],"why":"Simultaneous variations of one melody is heterophony."},{"q":"A barbershop quartet sings all four parts in the same rhythm, in block chords. That's…","a":"chordal homophony (homorhythm)","w":["melody and accompaniment","imitative polyphony","heterophony"],"why":"Same rhythm in every voice, one tune on top: chordal homophony."},{"q":"A round, where voices enter one after another singing the same melody, is…","a":"imitative polyphony","w":["free polyphony","heterophony","chordal homophony"],"why":"A round is a canon, the strictest kind of imitative polyphony."},{"q":"What was organum?","a":"early Western polyphony: a voice added to a plainchant","w":["a kind of organ","a monophonic chant","a dance rhythm"],"why":"Organum added a second voice to chant, first in parallel 4ths or 5ths, then more independently."},{"q":"A repeating figure that forms a layer, like a bass riff or a loop, is called an…","a":"ostinato","w":["antiphony","hocket","organum"],"why":"Ostinato, riff and loop all name a repeating figure that forms a layer."},{"q":"A melody's notes are passed back and forth between voices, a few at a time. That's…","a":"hocket","w":["antiphony","doubling","a drone"],"why":"Hocket splits one melody between voices; antiphony alternates whole phrases between groups."},{"q":"Ligeti's music packs so many lines together that they blur into a shimmer. That's called…","a":"sound mass (micropolyphony)","w":["monophony","call and response","build-up"],"why":"So many lines that they blur into one whole: sound mass, or micropolyphony."},{"q":"What does texture describe?","a":"how the layers of a piece fit together","w":["how fast the music goes","how loud the music is","which key the music is in"],"why":"Texture is about how many layers there are and how they relate."},{"q":"What are the four basic textures?","a":"monophony, heterophony, homophony and polyphony","w":["solo, duet, trio and quartet","melody, harmony, rhythm and form","major, minor, modal and atonal"],"why":"These four describe how the layers relate."},{"q":"Men and women sing the same melody an octave apart. The texture is…","a":"monophony","w":["heterophony","homophony","polyphony"],"why":"Octave doubling is still one line."},{"q":"Hildegard of Bingen's chants are examples of…","a":"monophony","w":["polyphony","homophony","heterophony"],"why":"The earliest notated Western music is monophonic."},{"q":"How do you recognize heterophony?","a":"one tune decorated differently by several performers at once","w":["two different tunes at once","a melody over chords","one voice alone"],"why":"Heterophony is one melody, varied simultaneously."},{"q":"Which tradition is heterophony central to?","a":"Javanese and Balinese gamelan","w":["Bach fugues","barbershop quartets","Gregorian chant"],"why":"Gamelan, Middle Eastern ensembles and fiddle music all use it."},{"q":"A singer with strummed guitar chords is…","a":"homophony (melody and accompaniment)","w":["polyphony","monophony","heterophony"],"why":"A melody over chords with their own rhythm is homophony."},{"q":"Since about when has homophony dominated much Western music?","a":"about 1600","w":["about 1000","about 1900","about 1400"],"why":"Homophony dominates most music since about 1600."},{"q":"Voices entering one after another with the same melody make…","a":"imitative polyphony","w":["free polyphony","homophony","heterophony"],"why":"Imitation passes the same melody between entering voices."},{"q":"A countermelody against a tune is an example of…","a":"free (non-imitative) polyphony","w":["imitative polyphony","monophony","chordal homophony"],"why":"Different simultaneous melodies make free polyphony."},{"q":"What does density describe?","a":"how many notes or layers sound at once","w":["how loud the music is","how fast the tempo is","how high the melody is"],"why":"Density runs from thin and sparse to thick and dense."},{"q":"Contrasting layers that seem to run independently are called…","a":"stratification","w":["doubling","call and response","a drone"],"why":"Stratification sets independent layers against each other."}]},{"id":"timbre","title":"Understanding Timbre","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"A flute and a violin play the same pitch, equally loud, for the same length. What lets you tell them apart?","a":"timbre","w":["pitch","dynamics","rhythm"],"why":"Timbre is what's left to tell two sounds apart when pitch, loudness and duration match."},{"q":"Which waveform contains every partial (1, 2, 3, 4…) and sounds bright and buzzy?","a":"sawtooth","w":["sine","square","triangle"],"why":"The sawtooth has all partials at 1/n strength. Square and triangle have odd partials only; a sine has just the fundamental."},{"q":"Why does an oboe sound nasal?","a":"Its 2nd–4th partials are stronger than its fundamental","w":["It has no overtones","It plays only odd partials","Its fundamental is very loud"],"why":"The oboe's middle partials outweigh its fundamental. A flute is close to a sine; a low clarinet favors odd partials."},{"q":"On a Hammond organ, pulling out more drawbars adds partials. What kind of synthesis is that?","a":"additive","w":["subtractive","FM","sampling"],"why":"Building a tone partial by partial is additive synthesis."},{"q":"In an ADSR envelope, which stage is the time a note takes to reach full level?","a":"attack","w":["decay","sustain","release"],"why":"Attack is the rise; decay falls to the sustain level; sustain holds; release fades after the note ends."},{"q":"A sound described as “dark” or “mellow” has…","a":"weak high partials","w":["strong high partials","inharmonic partials","noise mixed in"],"why":"Dark, mellow and warm sounds have little energy up high. Bright ones have a lot."},{"q":"Speeding up a recording of a voice makes a “chipmunk” sound. Why?","a":"the formants move up along with the pitch","w":["the formants disappear","the voice loses its fundamental","the envelope gets longer"],"why":"Speeding up shifts everything, formants included, so the vowels and the voice's color change."},{"q":"A clarinet's low (chalumeau) notes sound dark; its higher clarion notes sound bright. That's a difference of…","a":"register","w":["dynamics","tempo","texture"],"why":"Each instrument has distinct colors in each register."},{"q":"Which technique makes a string instrument sound glassy and bright?","a":"sul ponticello (bowing near the bridge)","w":["sul tasto (bowing over the fingerboard)","pizzicato","a practice mute"],"why":"Near the bridge is glassy and bright; over the fingerboard is soft and flute-like."},{"q":"A melody passed from instrument to instrument, a few notes at a time, so that timbre becomes part of the line, is called…","a":"Klangfarbenmelodie","w":["unison doubling","octave doubling","contrast"],"why":"Schoenberg named it in 1911, and Webern used it often."},{"q":"How do you pronounce timbre?","a":"TAM-ber","w":["TIM-ber","tim-BRAY","TAMB"],"why":"Timbre is pronounced TAM-ber."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of timbre's ingredients?","a":"the tempo","w":["the spectrum","the envelope","noise"],"why":"Spectrum, envelope, formants, noise and their changes over time make timbre."},{"q":"Which instrument's tone is close to a sine wave?","a":"the flute","w":["the oboe","the trumpet","the violin"],"why":"The flute has weak upper partials, close to a pure tone."},{"q":"A square wave contains…","a":"odd partials only, giving a hollow, clarinet-like color","w":["all partials","only the fundamental","only even partials"],"why":"Square and triangle waves have only odd partials."},{"q":"Building a tone by setting the levels of individual partials is…","a":"additive synthesis","w":["subtractive synthesis","sampling","frequency modulation"],"why":"Organ stops and Hammond drawbars work the same way."},{"q":"While you move the partial sliders, what stays the same?","a":"the pitch","w":["the tone color","the spectrum","the brightness"],"why":"Changing partial levels alters color, not pitch."},{"q":"Struck and plucked instruments like piano and guitar have no…","a":"sustain: they only decay","w":["attack","release","pitch"],"why":"Once struck or plucked, the sound only fades."},{"q":"In classic experiments, what made instruments hard to identify?","a":"removing the first fraction of a second (the attack)","w":["removing the last second","playing them louder","playing them higher"],"why":"The attack carries much of an instrument's identity."},{"q":"What does a low-pass filter do?","a":"removes partials above a cutoff, making the sound darker","w":["removes the fundamental","makes the sound louder","raises the pitch"],"why":"Cutting high partials reduces brightness."},{"q":"On acoustic instruments, what happens to brightness as you play louder?","a":"it goes up","w":["it goes down","it stays the same","it disappears"],"why":"Louder playing brings out higher partials."},{"q":"How do we tell vowels apart?","a":"by their formants, set by the shape of the mouth","w":["by their pitch","by their loudness","by their length"],"why":"Different mouth shapes move the formants, which we hear as different vowels."},{"q":"Why does a vowel stay recognizable across a melody?","a":"formants don't move when the pitch changes","w":["the pitch never changes","vowels have no overtones","the singer changes vowels"],"why":"Formants are fixed by mouth shape, not by pitch."},{"q":"What gives bells and cymbals their metallic, ambiguous pitch?","a":"inharmonic partials","w":["a pure sine tone","only even partials","a slow attack"],"why":"Partials that don't fit the harmonic series blur the pitch."},{"q":"Which of these is noise in a timbre?","a":"the breath in a flute","w":["the fundamental of a violin","the 2nd partial of a trumpet","a sustained sine tone"],"why":"Breath, bow scrape and hammer thumps add noise to tones."},{"q":"What does pizzicato do to a string instrument's sound?","a":"it's plucked instead of bowed, giving a percussive envelope","w":["it adds vibrato","it mutes the strings","it plays harmonics"],"why":"Plucking replaces the bow's sustained sound with a quick decay."},{"q":"Bowing over the fingerboard (sul tasto) makes the tone…","a":"soft and flute-like","w":["glassy and bright","buzzy","percussive"],"why":"Sul tasto softens the tone; sul ponticello brightens it."},{"q":"Doubling a melody an octave above adds…","a":"brilliance","w":["weight","noise","a new pitch center"],"why":"An octave above adds brilliance; an octave below adds weight."},{"q":"What happens when a flute and clarinet play a melody in unison?","a":"their spectra blend into a new color","w":["the melody becomes polyphonic","the pitch rises","the clarinet disappears"],"why":"Unison doubling creates a color neither has alone."}]},{"id":"note-values","title":"Understanding Units of Rhythm","section":"Foundations","qs":[{"q":"How many eighth notes fill the same time as one half note?","a":"4","w":["2","8","16"],"why":"A half note = 2 quarters = 4 eighths."},{"q":"In 4/4, how many beats does a half note last?","a":"2 beats","w":["1 beat","4 beats","½ beat"],"why":"Whole = 4 beats, half = 2, quarter = 1, eighth = ½, sixteenth = ¼."},{"q":"Which values have an open (hollow) notehead?","a":"whole and half notes","w":["quarter and eighth notes","only the whole note","every value shorter than a quarter"],"why":"Whole and half notes are open; quarters and shorter are filled."},{"q":"A note sits above the middle line of the staff. Its stem goes…","a":"down, on the left side of the head","w":["up, on the right side","down, on the right side","up, on the left side"],"why":"Above the middle line: stem down, on the left. Below it: stem up, on the right."},{"q":"Which rest sits on top of the 3rd line, like a hat?","a":"the half rest","w":["the whole rest","the quarter rest","the eighth rest"],"why":"Half rest: a hat on the 3rd line. Whole rest: a hole hanging from the 4th line."},{"q":"A bar of 3/4 is silent. What do you write?","a":"a whole rest (the bar rest), centered in the bar","w":["a dotted half rest on beat 1","three quarter rests","nothing at all"],"why":"The whole-rest symbol doubles as the bar rest, even where a whole note wouldn't fit, and it's centered."},{"q":"How many beats does a dotted half note last in 4/4?","a":"3","w":["2½","4","2"],"why":"A half note is 2 beats; the dot adds 1. 2 + 1 = 3."},{"q":"A note needs to last across a bar line. Which do you use?","a":"a tie","w":["a dot","a double dot","a beam"],"why":"A tie joins two notes of the same pitch into one sound, and ties can cross bar lines."},{"q":"Which time signature is compound duple?","a":"6/8","w":["2/4","3/4","3/8"],"why":"6/8 has two dotted-quarter beats. 2/4 is simple duple; 3/4 and 3/8 are simple triple."},{"q":"At ♩ = 120, how long does each beat last?","a":"half a second","w":["one second","two seconds","a quarter of a second"],"why":"120 beats per minute is 2 per second, so each beat lasts ½ second."},{"q":"An eighth-note triplet fits three eighths into the time of…","a":"two eighths (one quarter-note beat)","w":["three eighths","four eighths","one eighth"],"why":"Three triplet eighths take the time of two regular eighths: one beat in 4/4."},{"q":"How do you count four sixteenth notes on beat 1 in simple meter?","a":"1 e & a","w":["1 & a","1 & 2 &","1 2 3 4"],"why":"Sixteenths are “1 e & a.” Compound divisions and triplets are “1 & a.”"},{"q":"What is the British name for a quarter note?","a":"crotchet","w":["minim","quaver","semibreve"],"why":"Whole = semibreve, half = minim, quarter = crotchet, eighth = quaver."},{"q":"How many sixteenth notes equal one quarter note?","a":"4","w":["2","8","16"],"why":"Quarter = 2 eighths = 4 sixteenths."},{"q":"How many flags does a sixteenth note have?","a":"two","w":["one","three","none"],"why":"Eighths have one flag, sixteenths two."},{"q":"Why do beams replace flags?","a":"to group notes by beat, so the meter is easy to see","w":["to make notes louder","to show the notes are tied","to mark the melody"],"why":"Beams group shorter notes into beats."},{"q":"Which rest hangs from the 4th line, like a hole?","a":"the whole rest","w":["the half rest","the quarter rest","the eighth rest"],"why":"The half rest sits on the 3rd line; the whole rest hangs from the 4th."},{"q":"In 4/4, beats 2 and 3 are silent. How should you write them?","a":"two quarter rests","w":["one half rest","a whole rest","a dotted half rest"],"why":"A half rest across beats 2–3 would hide the middle of the bar."},{"q":"What marks the very end of a piece?","a":"a final bar line (thin–thick)","w":["a double bar line (two thin lines)","a bar rest","a fermata"],"why":"A double bar marks the end of a section; thin–thick ends the piece."},{"q":"Several silent bars in a row in a part become…","a":"a multimeasure rest with the count above it","w":["a row of whole rests","an empty staff","a repeat sign"],"why":"A multi-bar rest saves space and is easy to count."},{"q":"How many beats does a double-dotted half note last in 4/4?","a":"3½","w":["3","4","2½"],"why":"The second dot adds half of the first dot's value."},{"q":"What can a tie do that a dot can't?","a":"cross a bar line","w":["make a note shorter","join two different pitches","change the meter"],"why":"Ties join same-pitch notes across bar lines (and across beats)."},{"q":"What is the beat unit in 9/8?","a":"the dotted quarter","w":["the eighth note","the quarter note","the half note"],"why":"9/8 is compound triple: three dotted-quarter beats."},{"q":"3/8 is…","a":"simple triple, with three eighth-note beats","w":["compound duple","compound triple","simple duple"],"why":"3/8 has three eighth-note beats, each dividing in two."},{"q":"Does a time signature tell you the tempo?","a":"no: speed is marked separately","w":["yes: 3/4 is always slow","yes: 4/4 is always 120 BPM","only in compound meter"],"why":"The time signature says how beats are grouped, not how fast they go."},{"q":"What does “M.M.” in an old score stand for?","a":"Maelzel's Metronome","w":["moderately moving","measure marker","metronome minimum"],"why":"Johann Maelzel patented the mechanical metronome in 1815."},{"q":"A quarter-note triplet fills…","a":"two beats","w":["one beat","three beats","half a beat"],"why":"Three quarter-note triplets take the time of two quarters."},{"q":"In 6/8, two eighths in one dotted-quarter beat are a…","a":"duplet","w":["triplet","quintuplet","dotted rhythm"],"why":"Duplets put two notes where compound meter normally has three."},{"q":"How is a beat of eighths counted in compound meter?","a":"1 & a","w":["1 & 2 &","1 e & a","1 2 3"],"why":"Compound beats divide in three: 1 & a."},{"q":"In the rhythm grid, what does each box stand for?","a":"one sixteenth note","w":["one beat","one eighth note","one bar"],"why":"Each box is a sixteenth; a note lasts until the next box that's on."},{"q":"Notes that happen at the same moment on different staves should…","a":"line up vertically","w":["be spaced evenly regardless of time","be written in the same color","always share a beam"],"why":"Vertical alignment shows what sounds together."},{"q":"How does horizontal spacing relate to time?","a":"longer values get more room, though not strictly in proportion","w":["every note gets the same space","shorter values get more room","spacing doesn't matter"],"why":"Spacing follows time loosely, keeping the beats visible."},{"q":"People say a bar of 6/8 has “six beats.” What are the six, really?","a":"the eighth-note divisions: there are two beats","w":["six quarter-note beats","six dotted-quarter beats","three beats of two eighths each"],"why":"In compound meter the top number counts divisions; 6/8 has two dotted-quarter beats."},{"q":"At a fast tempo, how is a bar of 3/8 usually felt?","a":"in one: a single dotted-quarter beat","w":["in three eighth-note beats","in two beats","in six"],"why":"Slow or moderate 3/8 is simple triple; fast 3/8 is felt as one beat per bar."}]},{"id":"pitch-sets","title":"Composing with Pitch Sets","section":"Advanced Harmony","qs":[{"q":"Why might a composer build a piece from pitch sets?","a":"to get a consistent vocabulary of melody and harmony without relying on triads","w":["to guarantee the piece stays in one key","to avoid repeating any interval","to make the rhythm simpler"],"why":"A set's intervals give the music a recognizable sound, the way triads do in tonal music."},{"q":"What is the pitch-class number of F♯?","a":"6","w":["5","7","8"],"why":"C 0, C♯ 1, D 2, E♭ 3, E 4, F 5, F♯ 6."},{"q":"What is 9 + 5 in pitch-class arithmetic?","a":"2","w":["14","4","11"],"why":"14 − 12 = 2: a perfect 4th above A is D."},{"q":"Transpose {0, 1, 4} up 3 half steps. What do you get?","a":"{3, 4, 7}","w":["{0, 3, 4}","{3, 4, 6}","{1, 2, 5}"],"why":"0+3, 1+3, 4+3 = {3, 4, 7}: E♭–E–G."},{"q":"Which set is the inversion of {0, 1, 4} (12 − x)?","a":"{0, 11, 8}, the same as {8, 11, 0}","w":["{0, 1, 4}","{4, 5, 8}","{1, 2, 5}"],"why":"0, 11, 8: C, B, A♭. Its intervals are those of {0, 1, 4} turned upside down."},{"q":"C–E–F belongs to which set class?","a":"3-4 (015)","w":["3-3 (014)","3-11 (037)","3-5 (016)"],"why":"{0, 4, 5} inverts to {0, 8, 7} = {7, 8, 0}, which starts on 0 as {0, 1, 5}."},{"q":"Which trichord set class contains both the major and the minor triad?","a":"3-11 (037)","w":["3-12 (048)","3-10 (036)","3-9 (027)"],"why":"Inversion turns C–E–G into C–A♭–F, an F minor triad: one set class."},{"q":"Which set is a major 3rd divided by a half step?","a":"3-3 (014)","w":["3-11 (037)","3-12 (048)","3-9 (027)"],"why":"0–1–4: a half step and a minor 3rd together span a major 3rd."},{"q":"Which is a way to compose with a pitch set?","a":"spin melodic cells from its transpositions and inversions, and stack it as chords","w":["use each note only once in the whole piece","always play the set in ascending order","avoid repeating its intervals"],"why":"The ear recognizes the set's interval shape in any position, as melody or chord."}]},{"id":"chromatic-chords","title":"Understanding Chromatic Chords","section":"Advanced Harmony","qs":[{"q":"Mode mixture borrows chords from…","a":"the parallel key (same tonic)","w":["the relative key (same key signature)","the dominant key","any key at all"],"why":"Mixture uses chords from the key with the same tonic, without modulating."},{"q":"In D major, how do you label a B♭ major chord?","a":"{rn:♭VI}","w":["{rn:vi}","{rn:VI}","{rn:♭vi}"],"why":"B is 6 in D major; B♭ is the lowered 6th, and the chord is major: ♭VI."},{"q":"In D major, G minor replaces G major. What is it?","a":"mode mixture: {rn:iv}","w":["a secondary dominant","a secondary leading-tone chord","the Neapolitan"],"why":"IV → iv: borrowed from D minor."},{"q":"A piece in D minor ends on a D major chord. That's a…","a":"Picardy third","w":["Mercury third","Neapolitan","chromatic mediant"],"why":"A Picardy third is a major tonic ending a minor-key phrase or piece."},{"q":"In D minor, what notes make up the Neapolitan chord?","a":"E♭–G–B♭","w":["E–G♯–B","E–G–B♭","D♭–F–A♭"],"why":"2 is E; lowered, E♭. A major triad on E♭: E♭–G–B♭."},{"q":"Which note is in the bass of a Neapolitan sixth (♭II⁶)?","a":"scale degree 4","w":["♭2","the leading tone","scale degree 5"],"why":"The chord's 3rd is in the bass, and that's 4 of the key (G in D minor)."},{"q":"Where does the Neapolitan usually go?","a":"to V (sometimes through i⁶₄ or vii°7/V)","w":["back to i","to ♭VI","to IV"],"why":"♭II⁶ moves to V, often with a chord in between to smooth the melodic diminished 3rd."},{"q":"Which two scale degrees form the augmented 6th in every augmented sixth chord?","a":"le (♭6) in the bass and fi (♯4) above","w":["do and sol","ti and do","re and fa"],"why":"le and fi close in on sol from both sides and open out to an octave on the dominant."},{"q":"In E minor, what is the Italian sixth?","a":"C–E–A♯","w":["C–E–F♯–A♯","C–E–G–A♯","C–E–G–B"],"why":"le = C, do = E, fi = A♯. French would add F♯ (re); German would add G (me)."},{"q":"Why does a German sixth often go through i⁶₄ before V?","a":"to avoid parallel perfect 5ths","w":["to avoid a diminished 3rd","because it's in a major key","to make it louder"],"why":"Ger+6 → V directly makes parallel 5ths; the cadential ⁶₄ (or a French sixth) avoids them."},{"q":"A German sixth sounds identical to which other chord?","a":"a dominant 7th on the same root","w":["a diminished 7th","a minor 7th","a major 7th"],"why":"A♭–C–E♭–F♯ and A♭–C–E♭–G♭ sound the same; the spelling shows where the note goes."},{"q":"Which chord is a chromatic mediant of G?","a":"E♭ major","w":["E minor","B minor","D major"],"why":"E♭ (a major 3rd below) shares G with G major. Em and Bm are diatonic; D is a 5th away."},{"q":"Using the raised leading tone in a minor key is…","a":"not mode mixture: that note already belongs to the key","w":["mode mixture","a Neapolitan","a modulation"],"why":"The raised 7th and melodic minor notes belong to minor already."},{"q":"In G major, how do you label E♭?","a":"{rn:♭VI}","w":["{rn:vi}","{rn:VI}","{rn:♭vi}"],"why":"E♭ is built on lowered 6, and it's major: ♭VI."},{"q":"Every borrowed chord contains at least one note from the parallel minor. Which notes?","a":"me (♭3), le (♭6) or te (♭7)","w":["di, ri or fi","ti, do or re","sol, la or ti"],"why":"Borrowed chords bring in me, le or te."},{"q":"In G major, A major replaces A minor. What is it most likely to be?","a":"a secondary dominant (V/V)","w":["a borrowed chord","a Neapolitan","a Picardy third"],"why":"A minor chord made major usually makes a secondary dominant."},{"q":"How is a Picardy third labeled?","a":"{rn:I}","w":["{rn:i}","{rn:♯i}","{rn:III}"],"why":"A major tonic in a minor key is labeled I."},{"q":"What is a “Mercury third”?","a":"a minor tonic arriving in a major-key context","w":["a major tonic at the end of a minor piece","a chord built on ♭3","a chromatic mediant"],"why":"Named after Freddie Mercury: “We Are the Champions” ends its chorus on one."},{"q":"What are the three stages of spelling a Neapolitan in any key?","a":"find 2, lower it a half step keeping its letter, build a major triad on it","w":["find 5, raise it, build a minor triad","find 4, lower it, build a diminished triad","find 7, lower it, build a dominant 7th"],"why":"Lowered 2, major triad: in A minor, B♭–D–F."},{"q":"Which other pre-dominants share the Neapolitan sixth's bass note?","a":"iv and ii⁶","w":["V and vii°","I and vi","♭VI and ♭VII"],"why":"♭II⁶, iv and ii⁶ all have 4 in the bass."},{"q":"What melodic interval can appear when ♭2 falls to the leading tone?","a":"a diminished 3rd","w":["an augmented 2nd","a perfect 4th","a major 2nd"],"why":"Composers often fill it in with i⁶₄ (♭2 → 1 → 7)."},{"q":"In a major key, the Neapolitan needs which two lowered notes?","a":"♭2 and ♭6","w":["♭3 and ♭7","♭2 and ♭3","♭6 and ♭7"],"why":"♭2 is the root; ♭6 (borrowed) is its 5th."},{"q":"In A minor, Bm/D has the same bass as ♭II⁶. What is it?","a":"{rn:ii6}","w":["{rn:♭II6}","{rn:V/V}","{rn:IV}"],"why":"Its root is the diatonic 2 (B), so it's ii⁶, not the Neapolitan."},{"q":"A major chord on the diatonic 2 is…","a":"{rn:V/V}","w":["{rn:♭II}","{rn:ii}","{rn:♭VI}"],"why":"A major triad on 2 (not ♭2) is a secondary dominant."},{"q":"Why aren't augmented sixth chords really tertian?","a":"they're built around one interval, not stacked in 3rds","w":["they have only two notes","they're always in second inversion","they contain no 6th"],"why":"They're named for and built around the augmented 6th."},{"q":"Which augmented sixth contains le, do, re and fi?","a":"French","w":["Italian","German","Neapolitan"],"why":"French = le + do + re + fi."},{"q":"Where do augmented sixth chords usually go?","a":"to V, or to the cadential six-four and then V","w":["to IV","to ii","to the Neapolitan"],"why":"le and fi close in on sol from both sides."},{"q":"What tells you whether that sound is a German sixth or a dominant 7th?","a":"the spelling: fi rises, a lowered 7th falls","w":["the tempo","the bass note","the dynamics"],"why":"F♯ (fi) leads up; G♭ (the 7th) leads down."},{"q":"In C major, where does the German sixth get me (E♭) and le (A♭)?","a":"they're borrowed from C minor","w":["from G major","from the Neapolitan","from the Dorian mode"],"why":"In major keys, the augmented sixth's lowered notes come from the parallel minor."},{"q":"A common jazz relative of the augmented sixth is…","a":"the tritone substitution","w":["the Picardy third","the plagal cadence","the Neapolitan"],"why":"Its tritone resolves just like the augmented sixth's frame."},{"q":"Which pair is a chromatic mediant relationship?","a":"C major and A♭ major","w":["C major and A minor","C major and E minor","C major and G major"],"why":"C and A♭ are both major, a major 3rd apart, sharing C."},{"q":"How many chromatic mediants does every major or minor chord have?","a":"four","w":["two","three","six"],"why":"Same quality, roots a M3 or m3 above or below."},{"q":"How many notes does a chord share with any of its chromatic mediants?","a":"exactly one","w":["none","two","all three"],"why":"C–E–G and A♭–C–E♭ share only C; C–E–G and E–G♯–B share only E. That single common tone is what links the two chords."},{"q":"In a polychord symbol, what does the horizontal line mean?","a":"one complete chord is stacked above another","w":["the lower note is the bass only","play the two chords one after the other","the chord is in second inversion"],"why":"A horizontal line stacks two whole chords; a diagonal slash names one chord and a bass note."},{"q":"In C major, F/G puts which notes together?","a":"G–F–A–C","w":["F–A–C–G (an F chord in inversion)","G–B–D–F","F–G–A–C–E"],"why":"G under F–A–C: a G chord with 7th, 9th and 11th and no 3rd, often called a sus9 chord."},{"q":"A C♯ diminished 7th sounding over a held D in D minor is…","a":"a dim7 chord over a tonic pedal","w":["a Neapolitan","an augmented sixth chord","a Picardy third"],"why":"The dim7 a half step below the pedal note leans on the tonic it sits over, as in the opening of Bach's Toccata in D minor."},{"q":"C/D (a C triad over D) contains D–C–E–G. Which chord is that?","a":"D9(sus4)","w":["Dm7","D7","C(add9)"],"why":"C is the 7th, E the 9th and G the 4th; with no F♯ there's no 3rd."}]},{"id":"extended-and-altered-chords","title":"Understanding Extended and Altered Chords","section":"Advanced Harmony","qs":[{"q":"What is the 9th of a C chord?","a":"D","w":["B♭","F","A"],"why":"C–E–G–B♭–D: the 9th is D (a 2nd plus an octave)."},{"q":"Why is the 13th the highest extension?","a":"Another 3rd above it would repeat the root","w":["Pianists can't reach higher","The 15th is too dissonant","Chord symbols stop at two digits"],"why":"A 3rd above A (the 13th) is C: the root again."},{"q":"Cø7 is another name for…","a":"Cm7(♭5)","w":["Cdim7","Cm(maj7)","C7(♭5)"],"why":"C–E♭–G♭–B♭ is half-diminished, also written Cm7(♭5)."},{"q":"What's the difference between C6 and C13?","a":"C6 has no 7th; C13 has the 7th","w":["C6 is minor; C13 is major","They're identical","C13 has no 3rd"],"why":"C6 is C–E–G–A. C13 has B♭ (and implies the 9th and 11th) under its A."},{"q":"What notes make up Csus2?","a":"C–D–G","w":["C–E–G–D","C–F–G","C–D–E–G"],"why":"The major 2nd (D) replaces the 3rd: C–D–G. Cadd2 keeps the 3rd: C–D–E–G."},{"q":"Unless the symbol says maj7 or m, what kind of seventh chord does an extended chord like D11 sit on?","a":"a dominant seventh","w":["a major seventh","a minor seventh","a half-diminished seventh"],"why":"Plain 9, 11 and 13 symbols build on a dominant 7th."},{"q":"What is the ♭9 of G7?","a":"A♭","w":["A","F♯","B♭"],"why":"The ♭ shows the direction of the change: A → A♭."},{"q":"In a chord symbol, what goes in parentheses?","a":"only alterations (and omissions)","w":["the 7th","every extension","the bass note"],"why":"Unaltered extensions replace the 7 (C13); only ♭/♯ tones and omissions go in parentheses: C7(♭9/♯11)."},{"q":"When players thin out an extended chord, which tones stay?","a":"the 3rd and 7th","w":["the root and 5th","the 9th and 11th","only the root"],"why":"The 5th, lower extensions and even the root can go; the 3rd and 7th define the quality."},{"q":"On a lead sheet, N.C. means…","a":"no chord: chord players stop until the next symbol","w":["natural chord","next chorus","no change: keep the same chord"],"why":"N.C. is “no chord.” The melody plays alone, or the band plays in unison, or a soloist fills."},{"q":"A series of dominant 7th chords slides down by half steps instead of resolving. Moving a whole chord shape like that is called…","a":"planing","w":["tonicization","a circle progression","a plagal cadence"],"why":"The chords keep their color and slide: planing."},{"q":"Why is G♭/C not an inversion of G♭?","a":"C isn't a note of the G♭ chord","w":["Slash chords are never inversions","The chord has no 3rd","C is the root of G♭"],"why":"G♭–B♭–D♭ has no C; read from C, it's C7 with ♭5 and ♭9 and no 3rd."},{"q":"What is the 11th of a C chord?","a":"F","w":["E","G","A"],"why":"C13: C E G B♭ D F A: the 11th is F."},{"q":"What is the 13th of a C chord?","a":"A","w":["B♭","D","F"],"why":"The 13th is a compound 6th: A above C."},{"q":"What does Cmaj7 contain?","a":"C–E–G–B","w":["C–E–G–B♭","C–E♭–G–B♭","C–E–G♯–B♭"],"why":"maj7 means a major 7th above the root."},{"q":"What kind of chord is Cm(maj7)?","a":"a minor triad with a major 7th","w":["a major triad with a minor 7th","a half-diminished seventh","a minor seventh"],"why":"Cm(maj7) = C–E♭–G–B."},{"q":"What is the difference between Cadd9 and C9?","a":"Cadd9 has no 7th; C9 has B♭ and D","w":["they're identical","C9 has no 7th","Cadd9 has a ♭9"],"why":"Extended chords imply the 7th; added-tone chords don't have one."},{"q":"What is C5?","a":"a power chord: root and 5th only","w":["a C chord with a 5th in the bass","C with an added 5th","a C augmented chord"],"why":"C5 is just C–G."},{"q":"What does D11 imply?","a":"the 7th (C) and the 9th (E) as well","w":["only the 11th","a major 7th","no 7th"],"why":"An extended chord implies its 7th and the unaltered extensions below the highest one."},{"q":"What is the ♯11 of A♭7?","a":"D♮","w":["D♯","D♭","C♯"],"why":"The 11th of A♭ is D♭; raised, it's D♮."},{"q":"Which altered tones replace the perfect 5th?","a":"♭5 and ♯5","w":["♭9 and ♯9","♯11 and ♭13","the 7th and 9th"],"why":"♭5 and ♯5 replace the 5th; the others are added above."},{"q":"In what order is a chord symbol written?","a":"root, quality, highest unaltered extension, (alterations), (omissions)","w":["alterations, root, quality","quality, root, bass note","root, alterations, quality, extensions"],"why":"C13(♭9), for example: root + quality + extension + alterations."},{"q":"Which tone do performers usually leave out first from an extended chord?","a":"the 5th (unless it's altered)","w":["the 3rd","the 7th","the highest extension"],"why":"The unaltered 5th goes first; altered 5ths are always played."},{"q":"Why might pianists and guitarists leave out the root?","a":"the bass player plays it","w":["it clashes with the 7th","it's always an extension","they never play roots"],"why":"With a bass player present, the root is often left to the bass."},{"q":"How should extended chords be analyzed in this course?","a":"as root position","w":["always as first inversion","from the highest note","as two separate chords"],"why":"The more extended a chord, the less likely it's inverted."},{"q":"On a lead sheet, what do slashes and diamond noteheads in the staff show?","a":"the band's rhythm only, not pitches","w":["the bass line","the melody","the chord roots"],"why":"They show rhythm; the pitches come from the chord symbols."},{"q":"Moving a whole chord sideways while keeping its color is called…","a":"planing","w":["a retrogression","a tritone substitution","a turnaround"],"why":"The dominants slide down instead of resolving: planing."},{"q":"Read from the bass, G♭/C is a C7 with which alterations?","a":"♭5 (G♭) and ♭9 (D♭), with no 3rd","w":["♯5 and ♯9","♭9 and ♯11","♯5 and ♭13"],"why":"G♭ is a ♭5 above C; D♭ a ♭9."},{"q":"In the vamp, why is G♭13's 11th (C♭) left out?","a":"it rubs against the 3rd","w":["it's the same as the root","it's played by the bass","13th chords have no 11th by definition"],"why":"In a dominant 13th, the 11th usually clashes with the 3rd."},{"q":"Which notes do B♭m7 and G♭13 share in the vamp?","a":"B♭, D♭ and A♭","w":["only B♭","G♭ and E♭","none"],"why":"The bass moves down a 3rd while three notes stay."}]},{"id":"harmonic-succession","title":"Understanding Harmonic Succession","section":"Advanced Harmony","qs":[{"q":"Which two chords share pre-dominant function in major?","a":"{rn:IV} and {rn:ii}","w":["{rn:V} and {rn:vii°}","{rn:I} and {rn:vi}","{rn:iii} and {rn:V}"],"why":"IV and ii are pre-dominants. V and vii° are dominants."},{"q":"On the progression chart, each box leads to the box to its right. How are their roots related?","a":"each root is a 5th above the next one along","w":["each root is a step below","each root is a 3rd above","the roots are the same"],"why":"Moving right, roots fall by 5ths: iii → vi → ii → V → I."},{"q":"What does the arc on the chart, V going to vi, represent?","a":"the deceptive resolution","w":["a plagal cadence","a retrogression","a modulation"],"why":"V → vi instead of I is the deceptive resolution."},{"q":"Is ii → V a progression or a retrogression?","a":"a progression","w":["a retrogression","neither","only in minor"],"why":"ii sits left of V, so ii → V moves toward the tonic: a progression."},{"q":"In the phrase model, where can the pre-dominant area go?","a":"only to the dominant","w":["only to the tonic","anywhere","back to the tonic or to the dominant"],"why":"T → PD → D → (T). PD leads only to D."},{"q":"A circle progression's roots…","a":"fall by 5ths (or rise by 4ths)","w":["rise by step","fall by 3rds","stay the same"],"why":"iii – vi – ii – V – I: each root falls a 5th."},{"q":"Which of these is a retrogression?","a":"{rn:V} – {rn:IV}","w":["{rn:IV} – {rn:V}","{rn:I} – {rn:V}","{rn:vi} – {rn:V}"],"why":"V → IV moves right to left on the chart."},{"q":"The Andalusian cadence, i – ♭VII – ♭VI – V, is an example of a…","a":"chord stream down a tetrachord","w":["circle progression","plagal cadence","tonic prolongation"],"why":"Cm – B♭ – A♭ – G harmonizes a falling bass line, one chord per step."},{"q":"Which chord is likely to be more structural?","a":"a root-position diatonic chord on a strong beat","w":["an inverted chord on a weak beat","a chromatic chord inside a turnaround","a chord full of non-chord tones"],"why":"Diatonic, root position and strong beats all make a chord more structural."},{"q":"V⁴₃ between I and I⁶, with the bass walking up by step, is a…","a":"passing chord","w":["neighbor chord","pedal chord","cadential ⁶₄"],"why":"The bass passes from 1 to 3 through 2: a passing chord."}]},{"id":"modulation","title":"Understanding Modulation","section":"Advanced Harmony","qs":[{"q":"A phrase in C major visits D7 and G, then cadences back in C. Is that a modulation?","a":"No: it's a tonicization","w":["Yes: D7 is from another key","Yes: any chromatic chord is a modulation","No: it's a pivot chord"],"why":"Without a cadence in the new key, it's a tonicization."},{"q":"A new section simply begins in a new key, with no shared chord. That's a…","a":"direct (phrase) modulation","w":["pivot modulation","tonicization","chromatic mediant"],"why":"Starting a new phrase in a new key with no pivot is a direct modulation."},{"q":"Which key is closely related to G major?","a":"E minor","w":["E♭ major","F♯ major","C minor"],"why":"E minor is G's relative minor: same key signature. G's closely related keys are D, C, Em, Bm and Am."},{"q":"A phrase in C major ends with a half cadence on G. Has it modulated to G major?","a":"No: ending on V doesn't confirm a new key","w":["Yes: it ends on G","Yes, if the phrase is long","Only if G has a 7th"],"why":"A half cadence ends on V in the same key."},{"q":"Going from G major to D major, which chord could be the pivot?","a":"Em: vi in G, ii in D","w":["C: IV in G, ♭VII in D","F♯: in neither key as a major chord","E: V/V in G"],"why":"E minor is diatonic in both keys: vi in G, ii in D."},{"q":"To find a pivot chord, first find the first chord that doesn't belong to the old key. Then…","a":"step back one and check that it fits both keys","w":["go forward one","look for the tonic","count the sharps in the new key"],"why":"The chord just before the first chromatic chord is usually the pivot, if it's diatonic to both keys."},{"q":"Why can a fully diminished 7th chord lead to several different keys?","a":"Respelled, its four notes can act as four leading-tone sevenths, each resolving to its own tonic","w":["It contains every note of the scale","It has no root","It's always a pivot in closely related keys"],"why":"Each respelling gives a different root and leads to a different tonic a half step above it."},{"q":"I becomes I⁷ (C becomes C7) and leads to F. That's a…","a":"chord-quality shift","w":["common-tone pivot","direct modulation","truck driver's gear change"],"why":"Adding a minor 7th turns the tonic into V⁷ of IV."},{"q":"A song jumps up a whole step for its final chorus. That's a…","a":"truck driver's gear change","w":["pivot modulation","turnaround","tonicization"],"why":"A sudden direct modulation up a half or whole step, often before the last chorus."},{"q":"In a binary form in a major key, the first part usually ends in…","a":"the dominant key","w":["the subdominant key","the parallel minor","a distantly related key"],"why":"The first part usually ends in the dominant (or relative major, in minor), and the second part brings the music home."},{"q":"What must happen for a key change to count as a modulation, not a tonicization?","a":"a cadence in the new key confirms it","w":["the key signature changes","the melody leaps","the music gets louder"],"why":"A tonicization only visits; a modulation is confirmed by a cadence."},{"q":"A harmony that belongs to both the old and the new key is a…","a":"pivot chord","w":["turnaround chord","secondary dominant","passing chord"],"why":"A pivot chord belongs to both keys and links them inside a phrase."},{"q":"Which keys are closely related to C major?","a":"G major, F major, A minor, E minor and D minor","w":["C minor, E♭ major and A♭ major","D major, B minor and A major","only G major and F major"],"why":"The dominant, subdominant, relative, and their relatives."},{"q":"Why do modulations to closely related keys sound subtler?","a":"their scales share more notes","w":["they're always shorter","they use only major keys","they happen at the end of the piece"],"why":"Keys one accidental apart share six of seven notes."},{"q":"What's the most reliable way to find a modulation?","a":"compare a phrase's starting key with the key of its final cadence","w":["look for the highest note","count the accidentals in the first bar","look for a change of time signature"],"why":"If the cadence is in a different key from the start, the phrase modulated."},{"q":"How do you label a pivot chord in an analysis?","a":"with a bracket holding its numeral in both keys","w":["with a circle around the chord","with a fermata","with a new key signature"],"why":"The pivot bracket shows the old-key numeral above and the new-key numeral below."},{"q":"From C major to G major, Am can pivot as…","a":"vi in C and ii in G","w":["ii in C and vi in G","IV in C and I in G","iii in C and vi in G"],"why":"A minor is the 6th degree of C and the 2nd of G."},{"q":"A chromatic chord is the new key's V, preceded by its cadential ⁶₄. Where is the pivot?","a":"before the ⁶₄, since the ⁶₄ already belongs to the new key","w":["on the ⁶₄","on the V","there is no pivot"],"why":"The cadential ⁶₄ is part of the new key's cadence, so the pivot comes earlier."},{"q":"A C major chord is I in C major. What is it in A major?","a":"♭III, borrowed from A minor","w":["IV","V/V","vi"],"why":"A chromatic pivot: C is diatonic in C and borrowed (♭III) in A."},{"q":"An enharmonic pivot chord works by…","a":"respelling it so it resolves in a new key","w":["holding one note while the others change","repeating a melody higher","changing the meter"],"why":"A dim7 or German sixth can be respelled to point toward a different tonic."},{"q":"A common-tone modulation binds two chords by…","a":"one shared tone","w":["a whole shared chord","a sequence","a shared bass line only"],"why":"The common tone links chords whose roots are a chromatic 3rd apart."},{"q":"In a sequential modulation, the music changes key by…","a":"repeating a melodic gesture higher or lower over a moving harmonic pattern","w":["holding one chord for a long time","stopping and restarting","using a pivot bracket"],"why":"A sequence can pass through chords outside both keys on its way to the new one."},{"q":"Turnaround chords…","a":"come after a cadence and lead into the next phrase","w":["are pivot chords inside a phrase","always modulate up a half step","end the piece"],"why":"They keep the music moving, sometimes tonicizing the next phrase's key."},{"q":"Where does pop's “truck driver's gear change” usually happen?","a":"before the final chorus","w":["in the first verse","in the intro","in the bridge only"],"why":"A sudden step up often launches the last chorus."},{"q":"In sectional classical forms like binary and ternary, sections usually contrast in…","a":"closely related keys","w":["distantly related keys","no key at all","the same key throughout"],"why":"Sectional forms commonly use contrasting, closely related keys."},{"q":"In a minor-key binary form, the first part usually ends in…","a":"the relative major","w":["the dominant minor","the parallel major","the subdominant"],"why":"Major-key pieces go to the dominant; minor-key pieces usually to the relative major."}]},{"id":"secondary-function-chords","title":"Understanding Secondary Function Chords","section":"Advanced Harmony","qs":[{"q":"What is tonicization?","a":"briefly treating a chord other than the tonic as if it were the tonic","w":["changing to a new key with a cadence","playing the tonic chord twice","raising every note a half step"],"why":"Tonicization is brief; only a cadence confirms a new key."},{"q":"Stack B, D, E, G♯ in 3rds. What chord is it?","a":"E7 with B in the bass (E7/B)","w":["B minor 7th","G♯ diminished","E minor"],"why":"E–G♯–B–D is a dominant 7th on E; B is the bass, so E7/B."},{"q":"A fully diminished 7th chord turns up outside the key. What is it most likely?","a":"a secondary leading-tone chord","w":["a secondary dominant","a secondary pre-dominant","the key's own V⁷"],"why":"dim7 → secondary leading-tone chord (vii°7/x)."},{"q":"In G major, the chord G7 appears. What is it?","a":"{rn:V7/IV}","w":["{rn:I7}","{rn:V7}","{rn:V7/V}"],"why":"G7 is the dominant 7th of C, which is IV in G major."},{"q":"For a secondary dominant, where is the target's root?","a":"a perfect 4th above (5th below) it","w":["a minor 2nd above","a major 2nd below","a major 3rd above"],"why":"V's root is a 5th above I's, so the target sits a P4 above the secondary dominant's root."},{"q":"In G major, A7 is…","a":"{rn:V7/V}","w":["{rn:V7/ii}","{rn:ii7}","{rn:V7/vi}"],"why":"A7 resolves to D, the V chord of G major: V7/V."},{"q":"In G major, A7 with C♯ in the bass is labeled…","a":"{rn:V65/V}","w":["{rn:V43/V}","{rn:V42/V}","{rn:V7/V}"],"why":"3rd in the bass is first inversion: V65/V."},{"q":"In G major, which chord is {rn:vii°7/V}?","a":"C♯°7","w":["D♭°7","F♯°7","A7"],"why":"V is D. A half step below, spelled as its lower neighbor: C♯. C♯–E–G–B♭."},{"q":"Why spell the secondary leading tone as a lower neighbor (F♯, not G♭, below G)?","a":"It shows the note rises by half step to the target's root","w":["Sharps are easier to read","G♭ is not a real note","It makes the chord minor"],"why":"F♯ rises to G; an enharmonic G♭ would hide that."},{"q":"Does a secondary chord change the key?","a":"no: only a cadence can confirm a new key","w":["yes, always","only if it's a seventh chord","only in minor"],"why":"A secondary chord treats a chord as a temporary tonic without modulating."},{"q":"In V/x, what is x?","a":"the target: the chord being tonicized","w":["the bass note","the key","the inversion"],"why":"x is the chord the secondary chord points to."},{"q":"Stack A, C, F♯, E♭ in 3rds. What chord is it?","a":"F♯°7 (fully diminished seventh) over A","w":["Am6","A7","Cm7"],"why":"F♯–A–C–E♭ is a fully diminished seventh on F♯; A in the bass makes it inverted."},{"q":"After stacking a chord in 3rds, how do you show that it's inverted?","a":"add a slash and the bass note","w":["circle it","write it in lowercase","add a ♯"],"why":"If the lowest note isn't the root, write a slash chord."},{"q":"In C major, what does a D major chord (instead of D minor) suggest?","a":"a secondary dominant","w":["a borrowed chord","a secondary pre-dominant","a Picardy third"],"why":"A major triad where the key has another quality points to a secondary dominant."},{"q":"A half-diminished 7th outside the key usually signals…","a":"a secondary pre-dominant with a minor target (iiø7/x)","w":["a secondary dominant","a secondary leading-tone chord","a Neapolitan"],"why":"Half-diminished secondary chords are pre-dominants of minor chords."},{"q":"For a secondary leading-tone chord, where is the target's root?","a":"a minor 2nd above it","w":["a perfect 4th above","a major 2nd below","a perfect 5th below"],"why":"The chord's root is the target's leading tone, a half step below it."},{"q":"In C major, Gm7 going to F is…","a":"{rn:ii7/IV}","w":["{rn:V7/IV}","{rn:vii°7/IV}","{rn:iv7}"],"why":"Gm7 is the pre-dominant of F: ii7/IV."},{"q":"In C major, which label fits D7/A?","a":"{rn:V43/V}","w":["{rn:V65/V}","{rn:V42/V}","{rn:V7/V}"],"why":"Second-inversion seventh chords take 4/3."},{"q":"Where does the inversion figure go in a secondary label?","a":"after the numeral, before the slash","w":["after the slash","before the numeral","under the bass note only"],"why":"Figures attach to the first numeral: V⁶₅/V."},{"q":"A secondary leading-tone chord or pre-dominant usually leads next to…","a":"V⁷ of that same target, and then the target","w":["the home tonic","nothing: it ends the piece","a chord a tritone away"],"why":"E7 → C7 → F, or Gm7 → C7 → F."},{"q":"In C major, the leading tone of V (G) is spelled…","a":"F♯, not G♭","w":["G♭, not F♯","either way","E♯"],"why":"The secondary leading tone uses the letter below the target's root."},{"q":"How do you start spelling a secondary pre-dominant?","a":"a whole step above the target's root","w":["a half step below the target's root","a 5th above the target's root","on the tonic"],"why":"Pre-dominants sit a step above the target's root."},{"q":"In C major, which chord is V⁷/ii?","a":"A7","w":["D7","Am7","G7"],"why":"The dominant of D is A; A7 (A–C♯–E–G) is V⁷/ii. The C♯ is the chromatic note."},{"q":"Why is there no secondary dominant of vii° in a major key?","a":"a diminished chord can't act as a temporary tonic","w":["its root is already chromatic","it has no 5th","it's always in first inversion"],"why":"Only major and minor chords are tonicized; diminished chords (vii° in major, ii° in minor) aren't."},{"q":"In G major, V/vi (B) moves to C instead of Em. What still resolves as expected?","a":"D♯ rises to E, the 3rd of C","w":["B falls to E","nothing: it's a mistake","F♯ rises to G"],"why":"V/vi's leading tone D♯ goes up to E; E just belongs to a C chord instead of Em."},{"q":"Which quality does a secondary leading-tone chord almost always have?","a":"diminished or fully diminished","w":["half-diminished","major","dominant 7th"],"why":"vii°/x or vii°⁷/x: the root is the raised note a half step below the target."},{"q":"In C major, what is vii°⁷/V?","a":"F♯°7 (F♯–A–C–E♭)","w":["G♭°7 (G♭–B𝄫–D𝄫–F𝄫)","D7 (D–F♯–A–C)","F°7 (F–A♭–C♭–E♭♭)"],"why":"The secondary leading tone is F♯, not G♭; stack minor 3rds above it."},{"q":"What separates a common-tone dim7 from a secondary leading-tone chord?","a":"it shares a note with its resolution","w":["it's half-diminished","it's always in root position","it only appears in minor keys"],"why":"A CT°⁷ shares a note with its resolution; a secondary leading-tone chord shares none with its target."},{"q":"To spell ii/V in A major, where do you start?","a":"a whole step above E (F♯), building a minor chord: F♯–A–C♯","w":["a half step below E (D♯)","a 5th above E (B)","on A itself"],"why":"V in A is E. A whole step above is F♯; for a major target the chord is minor: F♯m."},{"q":"In C major, Cm7 – F7 – B♭maj7 is best labeled…","a":"ii⁷/♭VII – V⁷/♭VII – ♭VII","w":["i⁷ – IV⁷ – ♭VII","ii⁷/V – V⁷/V – V","iv⁷ – ♭VII⁷ – ♭III"],"why":"Cm7–F7–B♭ is ii⁷–V⁷–I in B♭, and B♭ is ♭VII (mode mixture) in C."}]},{"id":"articulation-and-phrasing","title":"Understanding Articulation, Dynamics and Phrasing","section":"Melody, Phrase and Form","qs":[{"q":"A dot over or under a notehead asks for…","a":"a short, detached note","w":["a held note","a louder attack","a smooth connection to the next note"],"why":"Staccato shortens the note, roughly to half its value."},{"q":"A short horizontal line over a note asks for…","a":"its full value, often with a slight lean","w":["half its value","a sudden loud attack","a pause"],"why":"Tenuto: held for its full length."},{"q":"Which mark asks for a stronger attack than the surrounding notes?","a":">","w":["a dot","a short line","an arc over a dot"],"why":"The accent mark points at the note that should stand out."},{"q":"A fermata tells the performer to…","a":"hold the note longer than written","w":["play it very short","play it twice","skip it"],"why":"How long is up to the performer or conductor."},{"q":"Marcato (^) compared with an ordinary accent is…","a":"heavier and a little separated","w":["softer","smoother","exactly the same"],"why":"Marcato is the strongest of the attack marks."},{"q":"Which dynamic marking means moderately soft?","a":"mp","w":["mf","pp","ff"],"why":"mp is mezzo-piano: moderately soft. mf is mezzo-forte: moderately loud."},{"q":"A narrowing wedge (>) under several notes asks for…","a":"a gradual diminuendo","w":["an accent on each note","a gradual crescendo","a pause"],"why":"A hairpin that closes is a diminuendo (decrescendo): gradually softer."},{"q":"Sforzando (sfz) on one note means…","a":"that note suddenly strong, then back to the level around it","w":["the rest of the passage loud","a gradual crescendo","hold the note"],"why":"It's a dynamic accent on a single note."},{"q":"A curve joining two notes of the same pitch is…","a":"a tie","w":["a slur","a phrase mark","a melisma"],"why":"The second note isn't played again; the two make one longer sound."},{"q":"A curve over several notes of different pitches asks for…","a":"legato: smooth and connected","w":["one long held pitch","short, detached notes","a pause"],"why":"For singers it usually means one syllable over all the notes."},{"q":"One syllable sung across several notes is called…","a":"a melisma","w":["syllabic setting","a tie","a fermata"],"why":"Melismas usually fall on stressed syllables and open vowels."},{"q":"A breath mark (,) above the staff shows…","a":"where a singer or wind player can breathe","w":["where to get louder","where the piece ends","a short note"],"why":"Breaths belong between phrases or words, not inside a word."},{"q":"Where does a phrase usually carry the most weight?","a":"at its goal, near the end","w":["on its first note","equally on every note","on the rests"],"why":"Phrases lean toward a goal, usually the high point or the cadence, and relax after it."},{"q":"Playing every note of a phrase at the same weight and length usually sounds…","a":"mechanical","w":["expressive","syncopated","legato"],"why":"Shape comes from small differences in weight, dynamics and time."},{"q":"Where do performers usually breathe or take a little time?","a":"between phrases","w":["in the middle of a word","before every beat","only at the end of the piece"],"why":"Breathing at phrase ends keeps the words and the musical sentence together."},{"q":"In a natural setting, the stressed syllable of a word usually falls on…","a":"a strong beat or a long note","w":["the shortest note in the bar","an offbeat sixteenth","a rest"],"why":"The beat gives weight; the stressed syllable should get it."},{"q":"In 4/4, which beats are strongest?","a":"1 and 3","w":["2 and 4","the offbeats","all four equally"],"why":"Beat 1 is strongest, beat 3 next; 2 and 4 are weaker, and offbeats weaker still."},{"q":"Putting “the” on beat 1 and the stressed syllable of the next word on a short offbeat usually sounds…","a":"awkward: the light word gets the emphasis","w":["natural","syncopated","legato"],"why":"Light words like “the” belong before the beat."},{"q":"Which syllable of “tomorrow” is stressed?","a":"the 2nd","w":["the 1st","the 3rd","none"],"why":"to-MOR-row."},{"q":"Emphasis that comes from a note's length is called…","a":"an agogic accent","w":["a dynamic accent","a staccato","a hemiola"],"why":"A long note draws attention even without extra volume."},{"q":"Melismas most often go on…","a":"stressed syllables with open vowels","w":["unstressed syllables like “the”","consonants","rests"],"why":"Open vowels (ah, oh) carry long notes well, and the stress deserves the time."},{"q":"A stressed syllable sung an eighth note before the beat and held through it is…","a":"a syncopation (a push)","w":["a misaccent","a fermata","a tie between phrases"],"why":"The early arrival gives the line forward motion; holding it keeps the stress."},{"q":"What makes a pushed stress sound natural rather than wrong?","a":"it's held across the beat","w":["it's very short","it's followed by a rest on the beat","it's sung softer"],"why":"A stressed syllable on a short offbeat note, with a weak syllable on the next beat, sounds misplaced."},{"q":"Which styles use pushed, speech-like stresses most often?","a":"conversational styles such as R&B, rap and country storytelling","w":["marches","hymns","Gregorian chant"],"why":"Hymns and marches mostly keep stresses on the beat."},{"q":"A line is set so every stressed syllable falls on a short offbeat note. How does it sound?","a":"the words sound mispronounced","w":["natural","pushed and lively","legato"],"why":"Stresses need either a beat or length; with neither, they get lost."},{"q":"Why do singers put a word's first consonant slightly before the beat?","a":"so the vowel lands on the beat","w":["to make the note shorter","to sing louder","to change the key"],"why":"The vowel holds the pitch, so it belongs on the beat."},{"q":"A diphthong such as the vowel in “light” is usually sung…","a":"on its first vowel, with the second added at the very end","w":["on its second vowel throughout","as two separate notes always","without any vowel"],"why":"Holding the first vowel keeps the tone open."}]},{"id":"motive-and-sequence","title":"Understanding Motive and Sequence","section":"Melody, Phrase and Form","qs":[{"q":"How are motives usually labeled?","a":"lowercase letters from the end of the alphabet: x, y, z","w":["capital letters: A, B, C","Roman numerals","numbers: 1, 2, 3"],"why":"x, y, z, with primes (x′) for changed versions. Capital letters are for sections."},{"q":"A motive keeps its rhythm, but its leap changes each time: an octave, then a sixth, then a unison. Which kind of motivic variation is that?","a":"a change of interval","w":["retrograde","diminution","real transposition"],"why":"The motive changes its interval, often to fit the harmony."},{"q":"A motive returns starting on another note, keeping its interval sizes (2nds, 3rds) but not all their qualities. That's…","a":"tonal transposition","w":["real transposition","inversion","augmentation"],"why":"Tonal transposition keeps sizes; real transposition keeps exact qualities too."},{"q":"Every interval of a motive changes direction: up becomes down. That's…","a":"inversion","w":["retrograde","augmentation","fragmentation"],"why":"Inversion flips contour; retrograde plays the notes backwards."},{"q":"A motive comes back with its notes in reverse order. That's…","a":"retrograde","w":["inversion","diminution","tonal transposition"],"why":"Reversing the note order is retrograde."},{"q":"A motive comes back with every note shorter. That's…","a":"diminution","w":["augmentation","fragmentation","extension"],"why":"Diminution shortens note values; augmentation lengthens them."},{"q":"A new motive starts as a piece broken off an earlier one, then develops on its own. That's…","a":"fragmentation","w":["inversion","retrograde","augmentation"],"why":"The new motive is a fragment that gains independence."},{"q":"A sequence keeps its exact intervals at each repetition, so accidentals appear and the key changes. That's a…","a":"real sequence","w":["tonal sequence","modified sequence","melodic sequence only"],"why":"Exact intervals = real sequence. Staying in the key, with some qualities changed = tonal."},{"q":"A melody repeats a pattern one step lower each bar, keeping to the notes of F major. It's a…","a":"tonal sequence","w":["real sequence","retrograde","modulation"],"why":"Because it keeps to F major's notes, some intervals change quality: tonal."},{"q":"Some passages repeat a whole gesture at a new pitch just to open a musical sentence. What do they lack that a true sequence has?","a":"a push along the phrase's path toward the cadence","w":["exact intervals","chords","a melody"],"why":"A true sequence uses transposed patterns to drive toward a cadence; these use them to begin a sentence."},{"q":"A motive goes E–A–C♯, then E–D–C♯–A. How is its second part related to the first?","a":"it's an inversion: up, then down","w":["it's the same notes repeated","it's a retrograde","it's an augmentation"],"why":"A rising figure answered by a falling one: inversion."},{"q":"What is a motive?","a":"a short, distinctive musical idea that keeps its identity","w":["a whole section of a piece","any repeated accompaniment pattern","the final cadence of a phrase"],"why":"A motive is small and distinctive: melodic, harmonic, rhythmic or a mix."},{"q":"Is an undeveloped ostinato usually treated as a motive?","a":"not usually: it's too nondescript to matter analytically","w":["yes, always","only if it's in the bass","only in Baroque music"],"why":"A nondescript, undeveloped figure isn't of much analytical interest."},{"q":"Besides starting as a brand-new idea, where can a new motive come from?","a":"a broken-off piece of an earlier motive","w":["only from the bass line","only from the chords","only from the lyrics"],"why":"A fragment of one motive can gain independence and become a motive of its own."},{"q":"Real transposition keeps…","a":"every interval's exact quality","w":["only the contour","only the rhythm","only the first note"],"why":"Real transposition reproduces each interval exactly (m3 stays m3)."},{"q":"Tonal transposition keeps…","a":"interval sizes, but not always their qualities","w":["every interval's exact quality","only the rhythm","nothing but the contour"],"why":"A 3rd stays a 3rd, but it may change from major to minor to fit the key."},{"q":"Retrograde means…","a":"playing the notes backwards","w":["turning the contour upside down","playing in longer note values","starting on a new pitch"],"why":"Retrograde reverses the order of the notes (and sometimes the rhythm)."},{"q":"The first half of a motive rises. If the second half is its inversion, the second half…","a":"falls","w":["rises again","plays the notes backward","moves in longer notes"],"why":"Inversion turns each rising interval into a falling one."},{"q":"Augmentation means…","a":"longer note values","w":["shorter note values","more notes","a higher octave"],"why":"Augmentation lengthens the rhythm; diminution shortens it."},{"q":"Why might a motive change its interval?","a":"to fit new harmony or a stepwise pattern of starting notes","w":["because the singer forgot it","to alter the meter","to avoid using the tonic"],"why":"Intervals bend to fit new chords or a sequence of starting pitches."},{"q":"If only the melody repeats at a new level, with changed harmony, it's a…","a":"melodic sequence","w":["harmonic sequence","real transposition","modified sequence"],"why":"When the harmony follows the pattern too, it's a harmonic sequence."},{"q":"A tonal sequence…","a":"stays in the key, so some intervals change by a half step","w":["keeps exact intervals and changes key","never repeats more than once","uses only perfect intervals"],"why":"Tonal sequences keep to the key; real sequences keep exact intervals."},{"q":"Which repetition of a sequence is most often modified?","a":"the third","w":["the first","the second","none: sequences are never modified"],"why":"The third repetition is the one most often adapted or embellished."},{"q":"When a whole gesture is transposed, like ii–V–I in D and then in G, what moves along with the melody?","a":"the chords","w":["nothing: the harmony stays put","only the bass line","the meter"],"why":"The whole gesture, harmony included, moves up a 4th."},{"q":"A motive returns with its intervals adjusted to fit new chords. How is it labeled?","a":"with a prime mark, like x′","w":["with a new letter","with a capital letter","with a number"],"why":"x′ is x with its intervals adjusted to new harmony."}]},{"id":"forms","title":"Understanding Musical Forms","section":"Melody, Phrase and Form","qs":[{"q":"In simple binary form, ||: A :||: B :||, does A come back at the end?","a":"No: A doesn't return","w":["Yes, always","Only in minor keys","Only if B is short"],"why":"Binary is two contrasting parts. A returning at the end of B makes it rounded binary."},{"q":"In rounded binary, how does the digression (B) usually end?","a":"on an open cadence, such as a half cadence","w":["on a perfect authentic cadence in the home key","without any cadence","on the same chord it started on"],"why":"The open cadence at the end of the digression leads into A′."},{"q":"A hymn uses the same music for every verse. That's…","a":"strophic form (AAA)","w":["through-composed","ternary","rondo"],"why":"Strophic form repeats the same music for each verse of text."},{"q":"In 32-bar song form (AABA), what is the B section usually called?","a":"the bridge (or middle eight)","w":["the chorus","the coda","the exposition"],"why":"A A B A: the 8-bar bridge contrasts, then A returns."},{"q":"ABACABA is a…","a":"rondo","w":["theme and variations","strophic song","compound ternary"],"why":"Rondo: A keeps returning between episodes B and C."},{"q":"An art song that follows a story with no repeated sections is…","a":"through-composed","w":["strophic","binary","rounded binary"],"why":"Through-composed music doesn't repeat sections; that's still a form."},{"q":"In a major-key sonata form, what key is the second theme in during the exposition?","a":"the dominant","w":["the tonic","the subdominant","the parallel minor"],"why":"Exposition: first theme in the tonic, transition, second theme in the dominant."},{"q":"In the recapitulation, the second theme returns in…","a":"the home key","w":["the dominant","a new key","the relative minor"],"why":"Both themes come back in the tonic."},{"q":"What's the difference between a refrain and a chorus?","a":"A refrain is a short repeated line at the end of each verse; a chorus is its own section","w":["They're the same thing","A chorus has different lyrics each time","A refrain is always instrumental"],"why":"A refrain lives inside the verse; a chorus is a separate recurring section, usually the main hook."},{"q":"A contrasting section, usually heard once before familiar material returns, is the…","a":"bridge","w":["verse","pre-chorus","outro"],"why":"The bridge contrasts and then leads back."},{"q":"||: A :||: B A′ :|| is…","a":"rounded binary","w":["simple binary","ternary","rondo"],"why":"Rounded binary returns to A's material at the end of B."},{"q":"Simple binary form is common in…","a":"Baroque dances and folk tunes","w":["Classical first movements","fugues","through-composed art songs"],"why":"Binary is typical of Baroque dances and of folk music."},{"q":"A minuet and trio ending with “M.D.C.” is an example of…","a":"compound ternary form","w":["simple binary form","strophic form","sonata form"],"why":"Minuet – trio – minuet, with larger sections, is compound ternary."},{"q":"In 32-bar song form (AABA), the B section usually ends with…","a":"a half cadence","w":["a perfect authentic cadence","a deceptive cadence","a plagal cadence"],"why":"The bridge contrasts and ends open, on a half cadence."},{"q":"How are sectional variations different from Baroque continuous variations?","a":"the theme and each new version are complete and end closed","w":["they have no theme","they use a repeating bass line","they change key every time"],"why":"Sectional variations are self-contained units; continuous ones flow over a repeating bass."},{"q":"Fugues, inventions and organ preludes are…","a":"contrapuntal forms","w":["strophic forms","rondo forms","binary dances"],"why":"They present a subject and add independent lines: contrapuntal forms."},{"q":"In a minor-key sonata form, the exposition's second theme is usually in…","a":"the relative major","w":["the dominant minor","the parallel major","the subdominant"],"why":"In minor the second theme area is usually the relative major."},{"q":"What leads from the development back to the tonic?","a":"the retransition","w":["the transition","the coda","the introduction"],"why":"The retransition prepares the return of the home key for the recapitulation."},{"q":"A passage that connects the verse to the chorus, often building tension, is the…","a":"pre-chorus","w":["post-chorus","link","bridge"],"why":"The pre-chorus links verse to chorus and builds toward it."},{"q":"A brief connecting passage, often instrumental, between larger sections is a…","a":"link","w":["refrain","pre-chorus","coda"],"why":"A link connects larger sections; it often shares music with the intro."},{"q":"Which plan is typical of a pop song?","a":"intro; verse–pre-chorus–chorus; verse–pre-chorus–chorus; bridge; chorus–outro","w":["A A B A, with an 8-bar bridge","exposition – development – recapitulation","A B A C A B A"],"why":"Pop songs repeat verse–pre-chorus–chorus units, then a bridge and a final chorus."},{"q":"A post-chorus is often…","a":"a vamp on the chorus's first chords","w":["a second bridge","a key change","a spoken introduction"],"why":"The post-chorus extends the chorus, often repeating a hook over its opening chords."},{"q":"In form letters, what does a prime (A′) mean?","a":"a varied return of A","w":["a brand-new section","a repeat sign","the first ending"],"why":"The same letter means the same music; a prime marks a varied version."},{"q":"A A′ A″ A‴ … describes…","a":"theme and variations","w":["rondo","strophic form","rounded binary"],"why":"Each prime is the theme changed again: theme and variations."}]},{"id":"phrases","title":"Understanding Phrases and Phrase Combinations","section":"Melody, Phrase and Form","qs":[{"q":"What marks a phrase most clearly?","a":"a melodic arch ending in a strong cadential gesture","w":["a change of time signature","a loud dynamic","a new instrument entering"],"why":"A phrase reaches a point of repose, most clearly with a cadence at the end of a melodic arch."},{"q":"A phrase comes back varied. How is it labeled?","a":"a′ (“a prime”)","w":["b","A","x′"],"why":"a′ is a varied return of a; b is contrasting; x′ is for motives."},{"q":"What's the difference between a phrase and a sub-phrase?","a":"A sub-phrase has too little harmonic progression to be a phrase; a phrase ends with a cadence","w":["Sub-phrases are always longer","A sub-phrase ends with a perfect authentic cadence","There's no difference"],"why":"Only the full phrase reaches a cadence; sub-phrases divide it into smaller harmonic motions."},{"q":"A sentence's presentation usually consists of…","a":"a short idea, then the idea repeated or sequenced","w":["two complete phrases","a cadence followed by a new idea","an antecedent and a consequent"],"why":"Presentation: idea + its repetition. Then the continuation leads to the cadence."},{"q":"In a period, how do the two phrases usually end?","a":"the second with a more conclusive cadence than the first","w":["both with perfect authentic cadences","the first with the stronger cadence","neither with a cadence"],"why":"The antecedent ends more open; the consequent closes."},{"q":"a (half cadence) → b (perfect authentic cadence) is a…","a":"contrasting period","w":["parallel period","parallel phrase group","double period"],"why":"Open then closed, with different beginnings: contrasting period."},{"q":"A period that modulates is called…","a":"progressive","w":["parallel","balanced","asymmetrical"],"why":"A progressive period ends in a different key from the one it starts in."},{"q":"Two phrases both end with authentic cadences and begin alike. What are they?","a":"a parallel phrase group","w":["a parallel period","a contrasting period","a sentence"],"why":"Both closed: a phrase group, not a period. Similar beginnings make it parallel."},{"q":"Four phrases where the cadence ending the first pair is weaker than the one ending all four form a…","a":"double period","w":["three-phrase period","phrase group","sentence"],"why":"That's the definition of a double period, as in Amy Beach's “Phantoms.”"},{"q":"What two qualities must a phrase have?","a":"some completeness, and a point of relative repose","w":["a melodic leap and a key change","at least eight bars","a repeated motive"],"why":"A phrase is complete and comes to rest at its end."},{"q":"What can blur where a phrase ends?","a":"elision, a missing melody, or an amorphous line","w":["a strong cadence","a melodic arch","a clear rest"],"why":"Elisions and unclear lines can hide the phrase ending."},{"q":"A phrase that can't be split into smaller units is…","a":"indivisible","w":["divided","a sub-phrase","a figure"],"why":"Divided phrases contain sub-phrases; indivisible ones don't."},{"q":"Small melodic units that are distinctive but not developed like motives are called…","a":"figures or gestures","w":["sub-phrases","periods","cadences"],"why":"Figures and gestures are distinctive but undeveloped."},{"q":"A sentence is typically how long?","a":"eight bars","w":["four bars","twelve bars","sixteen bars"],"why":"Idea, repeated idea, then a 3–4-bar continuation to the cadence."},{"q":"Which common pattern can be thought of as a sentence?","a":"the 12-bar blues","w":["the AABA song form","a minuet and trio","a fugue subject"],"why":"The 12-bar blues runs through fragments to one cadence at the end."},{"q":"In a period, the first phrase is the…","a":"antecedent","w":["consequent","continuation","presentation"],"why":"The antecedent is answered by the consequent."},{"q":"A period whose two phrases begin alike is…","a":"parallel","w":["contrasting","asymmetrical","progressive"],"why":"Similar beginnings make a parallel period."},{"q":"Two phrases belong together but both end closed. That's a…","a":"phrase group","w":["period","double period","sentence"],"why":"With no open first cadence, they form a phrase group, not a period."},{"q":"In a three-phrase period, which phrase has the most conclusive cadence?","a":"the last","w":["the first","the middle","they're all equal"],"why":"A three-phrase period saves its strongest cadence for the end."},{"q":"A piece's second phrase ends with an IAC in a new key. Why is that cadence weaker than one in the home key?","a":"it arrives somewhere other than home","w":["it's a half cadence","it has no melody","it's a deceptive cadence"],"why":"An arrival away from the home key is weaker than one back home."},{"q":"a (closed) – b (closed) is a…","a":"contrasting phrase group","w":["contrasting period","parallel phrase group","repeated phrase"],"why":"Two closed phrases with contrasting material form a contrasting phrase group."},{"q":"Which cadences count as weak?","a":"deceptive and imperfect authentic","w":["half and plagal half","perfect authentic and plagal","secondary half and half"],"why":"Open: half types. Weak: deceptive, IAC. Closed: PAC, plagal."}]},{"id":"contemporary-rhythm","title":"Contemporary Rhythmic Approaches","section":"Creating Music","qs":[{"q":"Several instruments repeat figures of different lengths, each at its own pace. What's the rhythmic effect?","a":"downbeats are hard to locate: the parts seem independent","w":["everyone plays a strict march rhythm","the music stops every bar","only one instrument plays"],"why":"With each part keeping its own pace, no shared downbeat stands out."},{"q":"Players who have been coordinating loosely by reading the score suddenly move into a steady 7/8. What changes?","a":"they lock together into one shared pulse","w":["they start improvising","the tempo halves","the key changes"],"why":"Loose coordination gives way to a common, measured pulse."},{"q":"Short 2/4 bars group into phrases of different lengths. What can stay constant underneath?","a":"a steady stream of eighth notes","w":["the phrase lengths","a 7/8 meter","the number of bars per phrase"],"why":"The bars group unevenly while the eighths keep going."},{"q":"Which grouping fits a bar of 7/8?","a":"2 + 2 + 3","w":["3 + 3","2 + 2 + 2 + 2","3 + 3 + 3"],"why":"2 + 2 + 3 = 7 eighths; so do 2 + 3 + 2 and 3 + 2 + 2."},{"q":"Quarter = 120. The triplet eighth becomes the new eighth. What's the new tempo?","a":"quarter = 180","w":["quarter = 80","quarter = 240","quarter = 120"],"why":"120 × ½ ÷ ⅓ = 180."},{"q":"In a metric modulation, five sixteenths become the new quarter. The new tempo is…","a":"four-fifths of the old tempo","w":["five-fourths of the old tempo","twice the old tempo","the same tempo"],"why":"Old × 1 ÷ 1¼ = old × ⅘."},{"q":"Additive rhythm builds music from…","a":"units of different lengths, so gestures keep changing length","w":["one repeated bar","notes that always fall on the beat","gradually slower tempos"],"why":"It can appear as changing meters or inside one meter."},{"q":"Ostinati of 6 and 10 sixteenths start together. When do they line up again?","a":"after 30 sixteenths","w":["after 16 sixteenths","after 60 sixteenths","after 20 sixteenths"],"why":"LCM(6, 10) = 30."},{"q":"Several percussion ostinati repeat, but each has a different length. Why does the texture keep changing?","a":"their alignment shifts with every repeat","w":["each player improvises","the tempo changes every bar","the meter changes every bar"],"why":"They line up again only after 240 sixteenths."}]},{"id":"minimalism-and-ambient","title":"Understanding Minimalist and Ambient Music","section":"Creating Music","qs":[{"q":"Minimalism began in the 1960s in…","a":"New York's Downtown scene","w":["Vienna's concert halls","Nashville's recording studios","the Paris Conservatoire"],"why":"It grew out of the Downtown New York scene of the 1960s."},{"q":"Minimalist music typically focuses on…","a":"a piece's internal process rather than motion toward a goal","w":["dramatic contrasts between sections","constant modulation to distant keys","virtuoso solos"],"why":"Steady pulse, consonance, repetition and gradual change put the process in the foreground."},{"q":"Performers work through a list of short cells, each moving on at their own pace, over a steady pulse. What does that create?","a":"overlaps that keep changing","w":["all the players in unison","free improvisation without notation","a conductor's bar-by-bar plan"],"why":"Each player decides when to go to the next cell, so the combinations shift."},{"q":"Two players clap the same pattern, and one keeps shifting it by an eighth note. Is that change sudden or gradual?","a":"sudden: one step at a time","w":["gradual: a slow drift in tempo","there's no change","it happens only once"],"why":"A fixed shift makes sudden steps; a slight tempo difference would make a gradual drift."},{"q":"In additive minimal music, a layer that fades in and out changes the texture…","a":"gradually","w":["abruptly","not at all","once per bar"],"why":"Fading layers blur the edges; abrupt ones make clear section changes."},{"q":"A mensuration canon is one where…","a":"each layer plays the same line at a different speed","w":["each layer plays a different melody","the layers change key in turn","the canon is improvised"],"why":"Every layer has the same line; only the rate changes."},{"q":"In a piece made of steady repeating patterns, what can bring important change without altering the patterns?","a":"shifting key signatures","w":["metric modulation","a drone","silence"],"why":"Key changes become events in an otherwise steady texture."},{"q":"Who popularized the term “ambient music”?","a":"Brian Eno","w":["Erik Satie","Philip Glass","La Monte Young"],"why":"Satie's “furniture music” was an early precursor."},{"q":"Compared with early minimalism, later (progressive) minimalism tends to…","a":"combine processes with intuitive choices and care about texture and color","w":["avoid repetition entirely","use only one layer","require tape loops"],"why":"Early pieces let one simple process run; later ones mix techniques."},{"q":"Which is a duration choice for a minimalist piece?","a":"cyclical, one complete cycle, sectional, or rounded","w":["always exactly three minutes","verse–chorus only","sonata form only"],"why":"Infinite pieces cycle; finite ones run one cycle, a series of sections or return to the opening."}]}];
